const e={"strong:0":{title:"Learning something new tends to give your energy a good direction.",gist:"Your strong energy does well with something new to learn and the patience to stay a beginner.",body:`Your chart is strong: it has plenty of energy of its own. A chart like this is helped by an outlet, somewhere for that energy to go, and for you Wood helps most. Saju links Wood with growth, learning and fresh starts.

In daily life, it might be a class where everyone knows more than you, a language you practise for ten minutes a day, or a skill you are happy to be bad at for a while. Being a beginner can also teach you to listen.

If you notice a wish to be good at everything at once, let growth be slow. A seed in a pot could be a daily reminder that growing takes its time.

Growth is slow, and you have all the strength it needs.`},"strong:1":{title:"Letting your energy show tends to keep it from building up.",gist:"Sharing your energy where people can see it helps most, as long as it leaves room for others.",body:`A strong chart carries a lot of energy, and it does best with somewhere to put it. For you, that outlet is Fire. Fire stands for warmth, expression and being seen: letting what is inside you out, where people can meet it.

It might look like teaching a friend something you know, hosting a small dinner, or making something and actually showing it. Kept inside, strong energy can turn into pressure. Shared, it tends to turn into warmth.

If you notice that you are doing most of the talking, ask one more question and wait for the answer. Warmth works best when it leaves room for others to shine too.

Let yourself be seen. There is room for your light.`},"strong:2":{title:"Simple routines tend to turn your big energy into something lasting.",gist:"Steady routines help your strong energy last, rather than spilling from one big plan to the next.",body:`Your chart is strong, and strong energy is helped by a channel, a way out that gives it shape. For you, Earth helps most. Saju links Earth with stability, care and routine: the ground that lets a big effort last.

In practice, it might be the same first hour each morning, a meal you make often, or a promise to yourself that you keep on busy days too. Small, repeated things are not exciting, but they hold.

If you notice yourself jumping from one big plan to the next, choose one and give it a regular time each week. Making your bed each morning could be a one-minute reminder of that steady base.

The strongest things are built one ordinary day at a time.`},"strong:3":{title:"Doing fewer things all the way tends to suit you best.",gist:"Clear limits and finished tasks help your strong energy most, though saying yes can come too easily.",body:`A strong chart can do a lot, and it is helped by an outlet that gives all that energy a sharp shape. For you, that is Metal. Saju links Metal with decisions, clear edges and finishing. A knife works because it is sharp, not because it is big.

It might look like a short list for the day, one clear no each week, or finishing what you started before you begin something new.

If you notice open tasks piling up, remember that each one takes a little of your attention. Close one before you open the next. A clear desk at the end of the day could be a small reminder of the habit.

Finish things, and let finishing feel good.`},"strong:4":{title:"Slowing down to think tends to help you more than pushing.",gist:"Rest, reflection and letting things flow help your strong drive, even when stillness feels like lost time.",body:`Your chart is strong, and a strong chart is helped by an outlet. For you, the most helpful one is Water. Water is linked with rest, depth and flow. It does not fight the rock; it goes around it and still reaches the sea.

It might look like a free afternoon with no plan, a slow walk with nowhere to be, or sitting by a window and watching the light change. Time to think can give your drive a deeper direction.

If you notice a feeling that rest is wasted time, try one unhurried hour a week and see what it changes. Pushing harder is not the only way forward.

Let some things flow. You can still get where you are going.`},"balanced:0":{title:"One goal, cared for a little each day, tends to suit you.",gist:"A small goal you care for each day keeps your easy balance from drifting.",body:`Your chart is balanced. It still leans a little one way, and Wood is the element that answers that lean and keeps you even. Saju links Wood with growth, plans and a direction of your own.

For you, that might mean one goal at a time: a song you learn line by line, a page of a book each night, or a skill you practise each morning before the day begins.

If you notice weeks passing while you wait for life to choose for you, pick something small and give it a little care every day. A mark on a calendar each day could be a simple reminder of how anything grows.

Choose one thing to grow this year, and see what it becomes.`},"balanced:1":{title:"Saying yes to what warms you tends to keep life bright.",gist:"Warmth and being seen keep your steady days bright, so say yes to what excites you.",body:`A balanced chart does not need fixing; it needs keeping even. In yours, Fire is the element that does that best, whichever way your chart leans. Fire stands for warmth, passion and being seen: the part of life that makes the heart beat faster.

It might be taking the microphone when it comes your way, sharing something you made, or telling a friend, in detail, about something you love. Small moments of being seen can warm a whole week.

If you notice yourself staying in the middle, where nothing can go wrong, remember that nothing can go wonderfully right there either. Try one small yes a week to something that excites you.

A brighter life can start with one small yes.`},"balanced:2":{title:"A few fixed points tend to give your flexible days a centre.",gist:"You adapt easily, so a few steady habits give you a centre to come back to.",body:`Your chart is balanced, and Earth is the element that helps keep it that way. Earth is linked with steadiness, trust and care: a centre to come back to when the days move around you.

It might be one slow meal you make on a quiet day, a regular call with someone steady, or a promise you keep to yourself each week. A few fixed points can hold you when everything else changes.

If you notice yourself saying “whatever works” to everything, choose one thing to keep the same for a month. Being flexible is a gift, and it works best with some ground under it.

Keep a few things the same. They hold you when life moves.`},"balanced:3":{title:"Finishing things tends to give your easy-going nature a clear shape.",gist:"You tend to see every side, so closing what is nearly done helps you most.",body:`In a balanced chart, the helpful element keeps things even, and for you that is Metal. Saju links Metal with decisions, structure and finishing: a clear line that gives an open, easy-going nature its shape.

It might look like reading the last chapter of a book left half-read, sending a message you have written in your head many times, or picking one plan and letting the others go.

If you notice a pile of almost-done things, choose one and finish it this week. Finishing is a kind of rest. A box ticked each evening could be a small reminder of the habit.

Close the chapters that are done. There are many pages still to write.`},"balanced:4":{title:"A little quiet thinking each day tends to keep you even.",gist:"Quiet time to think and write things down helps you most, though a phone can fill every pause.",body:`Your chart is balanced, and Water is the element that helps it stay that way. Water is linked with ideas, depth and quiet thinking: the slow part of the mind that notices where life is going.

It might be three lines in a notebook before bed, a long talk with no hurry, or a few minutes on a bench with nothing to do. Writing things down can show you, months later, a whole day you had forgotten.

If you notice every quiet moment filling up with your phone, leave one pause empty each day. Good thoughts often need a little silence to arrive.

Write down what you notice. Over time, it can read like a map.`},"weak:0":{title:"Fresh starts and kind company tend to help your ideas grow.",gist:"Moods can reach you easily, so quiet mornings and encouraging people help your ideas grow.",body:`A gentle chart grows best with support, and Wood is the element that gives it to you. Saju links Wood with growth, new leaves and the feeling of spring. Like a young tree, you tend to do well with good light and good company.

It might be the first quiet hour of a day, before anyone needs anything from you. Or friends who are learning something too, so you can grow side by side.

If you notice your mornings going to other people, or a new idea doubted before it has a chance, protect a little time for it and be kind to the idea first.

Something in you is still growing, and it deserves room.`},"weak:1":{title:"Warm rooms and glad faces tend to help you most.",gist:"You tend to take in a room’s mood, so warm places and kind people help most.",body:`Your chart is gentle, and a gentle chart is helped by support. For you, that support is Fire. Fire stands for warmth, light and being welcomed. You do not have to make all the warmth yourself.

It might be the one sunny seat in a café on a grey day, a friend who makes you laugh until you forget the time, or a group where people are glad when you arrive.

If you notice yourself hiding away when you are tired, remember that a little good company can warm you more than staying in. Say yes to warmth when it is offered.

You belong near the fire.`},"weak:2":{title:"A simple shape to your days tends to calm your worries.",gist:"A steady daily shape calms your worries, though you may look after yourself last.",body:`A gentle chart does well with support, and for you Earth gives the most. Earth is linked with steadiness, care and ground that holds you. You tend to feel everything around you, and steady ground helps it settle.

It might be a short walk around the block after dinner, the same breakfast most days, or a few quiet minutes outside. A worry that felt huge at six o’clock can be the right size again by the time you are back at your door.

If you notice you look after everyone first and yourself last, put your own name on the list too. Steady people can be part of that ground as well.

Look after yourself the way you look after the people you love.`},"weak:3":{title:"Clear, kind limits tend to give you room to breathe.",gist:"Other people’s needs can fill your days, so a kind but firm no helps you most.",body:`Your chart is gentle, and it grows best with support. Metal is the element that supports you most. Saju links Metal with order, clear edges and decisions. Limits are not walls; they are the frame that keeps a picture safe.

It might be twenty minutes spent clearing one drawer, an evening with your phone in another room, or a “not this week” said kindly and meant.

If you notice guilt after saying no, let it pass without changing your answer. The people who care about you can hear a no.

Draw your lines gently and clearly. There is room for you inside them.`},"weak:4":{title:"Rest without guilt tends to help you feel like yourself again.",gist:"Rest and quiet help you refill, though you may keep going long after you are tired.",body:`A gentle chart is helped by support, and Water gives you the most. Water is linked with rest, quiet and depth. You may take in a lot from the people around you, and needing to refill more often than others is not a flaw.

It might be a morning with no alarm, an evening with the lights low, or a long talk with one person instead of a crowd. Even an hour of quiet can do a lot.

If you notice yourself pushing through tiredness, try stopping one step earlier than usual. Rest is not giving up.

Like the tide, you come back.`}},o={"0:strong":{title:"Holding your ground tends to come easily, even over small things.",gist:"You stand firm and like to win, and you often make up with actions, not words.",body:`In a disagreement, you tend to stand firm. You trust your own way of seeing things, and being told what to do can make you hold on even tighter. Losing, even a small argument, can feel hard to accept.

It might be a debate about which way the station is, or whose turn it was to choose the film. Maybe you keep walking the way you chose, just a little faster. Afterwards you may make up with an action instead of words: a cold drink held out, or an offer to cook.

Some people, though, need to hear the words. Try saying “You were right about that” first, once in a while.

Standing firm and saying sorry can live together.`},"0:balanced":{title:"You can stay calm and friendly while quietly keeping your own way.",gist:"You stay calm and firm when others decide for you, but may leave hard talks too early.",body:`You tend to be easy to be with, until someone decides for you. Then you often turn calm and firm. You rarely shout. You simply keep your own space, kindly and clearly.

Maybe a weekend plan arrives with your name on every line, and you say yes to Saturday and keep Sunday free. Or a group picks a place to eat, and you join them for dessert. Once it is settled, you tend to let it go.

What can get lost is the harder talk. “Do what you want” may feel like an ending to you and like a closed door to the other person. It can help to stay a few minutes longer and say what you need.

Your calm helps most when you are still there.`},"0:weak":{title:"You tend to laugh along, then think about it later.",gist:"You keep things calm in the moment, but small hurts kept inside can grow bigger.",body:`In a tense moment, you tend to keep things calm and keep your feelings inside. You feel the mood around you clearly, and a small hurt may pass without a word from you.

It might be a joke about you at dinner that everyone laughs at, you included. Or a comment in a group chat that you answer with a smile. Later, on the way home or in bed, you may hear it again, now with better replies.

The flip side is that small hurts can grow in silence. One small habit: send one short, honest line while it is still small, like “That joke stung a little.” Said early, it is often easier to say kindly.

Your feelings count as much as the calm you keep.`},"1:strong":{title:"Being told to calm down tends to make you louder.",gist:"You argue with quick, sharp words, and your best line can hurt more than you meant.",body:`Your words tend to come fast in an argument. Rules that make no sense, or a flat “because I said so”, can make you push back hard. In the moment you are often quick, clear and very convincing.

It might start over which pasta to buy and end, five minutes later, as a speech about respect in the middle of a shop. Or a short “calm down” might turn a small talk into a long one.

The cost is that your sharpest line can stay with someone long after the topic is forgotten. Try counting to five before the line you are proudest of.

When you make up, you may do it with style: a silly bow, a song, a dramatic sorry. Add the quiet, plain one too.`},"1:balanced":{title:"You often reach for a joke when the room gets heavy.",gist:"Humour helps you calm a fight, though a joke can end the talk before it is solved.",body:`Humour tends to be your way through a disagreement. You may push back on a rule that seems silly, and you can also find the line that makes both of you laugh. A heavy silence rarely lasts long around you.

Maybe it is a defence of your messy desk that is half serious and half comedy. Or an apology drawn on a sticky note: two stick figures and a very sorry cat.

The flip side is that the laugh can arrive before the problem is solved, and the real question may still be there the next day. It can help to laugh first, then come back an hour later and finish the talk.

Let the joke lead to the talk, not replace it.`},"1:weak":{title:"Your best reply tends to arrive a few hours late.",gist:"In a hard moment your words can hide, and you often say them best in writing later.",body:`You often have plenty to say, but in a hard moment your words can go quiet. You may feel the other person’s mood so strongly that your own thoughts step back. They tend to return later, clear and well put, when you are alone.

It might be in the shower, on the bus or while brushing your teeth: the perfect answer, with nobody there to hear it. The next morning, you may write a careful message that is kinder than the one in your head.

What can happen is that the real talk only happens in your head. Try keeping one short sentence ready for the moment itself, like “That hurt. Can we talk tonight?”

Writing it later still counts. So does one true sentence now.`},"2:strong":{title:"You often know exactly whose turn it is.",gist:"You argue with facts about what is fair, and winning can leave the other person unheard.",body:`Fairness matters to you, and you tend to notice when things are uneven. Time, effort and money feel real to you. In an argument you often bring facts, dates and a clear sense of whose turn it is.

Maybe it is about the dishes, the last three taxi rides or who planned the last four trips. You may have the details ready while the other person is still looking for words.

The cost is that winning with numbers can make someone feel small. Sometimes a person wants to be heard more than corrected. Try asking “How was it for you?” before you bring out the facts.

You often make up by fixing things, with a plan that is fair. Now and then, let one small thing go.`},"2:balanced":{title:"You tend to turn an argument into a fair deal.",gist:"You solve a fight with a practical plan, though the feelings under it can go unsaid.",body:`When something goes wrong, your first question is often “What do we do now?” You tend to stay steady, look for a fair trade and move on. Most people welcome the plan.

It might be a friend running late for a film: you suggest the next showing, and they buy the snacks. Or a mix-up over a booking that you solve with two calls and a swap of days.

Sometimes the plan arrives before the feelings. You may still feel annoyed under the calm, and the other person may still feel bad. It can help to say one sentence about how you feel before you offer the deal.

A deal works best when both people feel heard.`},"2:weak":{title:"Small costs can pile up quietly before you mention them.",gist:"You often let small losses pass without a word, but they can add up in silence.",body:`You tend to look after your things, your time and your plans with care. When something small goes wrong with them, you may let it pass without a word.

It might be a borrowed book that comes back with a coffee ring. Or a shared bill where your part was a little too large, or a plan moved for the third time. You might say “It’s fine,” and mostly mean it.

The flip side: small costs can add up quietly, then come out all at once over something tiny. Try naming one small thing when it happens, kindly and plainly.

Your things and your time are worth one calm sentence.`},"3:strong":{title:"When you are truly upset, your voice tends to get quieter.",gist:"You keep your word and expect the same, but in a fight you can sound like a judge.",body:`Your chart points to someone who keeps their word and expects others to keep theirs. A broken promise can feel like a question of respect. In an argument you may become very calm, very clear and very hard to argue with.

Maybe someone said they would come at nine and arrives at eleven. You might say “It’s fine” in a voice so even that the room goes still. Or you might list, in order, what was agreed.

The flip side is that the other person can feel they are on trial, and naming your own part can be hard. Try saying your part first. It often makes the rest easier.

You tend to make up properly: clear words, a plan, and then it is truly over.`},"3:balanced":{title:"A fair fight, with a few rules, tends to suit you.",gist:"You keep arguments fair and calm, but the other person can feel managed rather than heard.",body:`For you, a good disagreement tends to be a fair one. One person talks, the other listens, and nobody shouts. You tend to stay steady under pressure, and you often help a talk get somewhere.

It might be a pause you suggest when a talk goes around in circles: “Let’s stop here and try again on Thursday, phones away.” Or two points you both agree on, written down before you go on.

Sometimes it can get a little too neat. Feelings can be handled like paperwork, and the other person may feel managed instead of heard. One small habit: ask “Is there anything I missed?” before you close the talk.

Keep the rules. Leave some room for the mess.`},"3:weak":{title:"You may say sorry first, even for the rain.",gist:"You apologise quickly when things go wrong, and you can carry blame that is not yours.",body:`You often take on more than your share of responsibility. When the mood turns bad, you may be the first to say sorry, and often the fastest. You also tend to feel the mood between people very clearly.

Maybe a day out goes wrong: rain, a closed café, a late bus. You might find yourself apologising for all of it, as if you had planned the weather. Or a small mix-up in a group ends with you saying sorry three times.

The cost is that you can carry blame that is not yours, and the other person may never see their own part. It can help to pause and ask yourself which part is truly yours.

Say sorry for your part. Only your part.`},"4:strong":{title:"Facts and sources are often your first move in a debate.",gist:"You argue well with ideas and facts, but being right can still leave someone hurt.",body:`In an argument, your mind is often your best tool. You enjoy ideas, and when something careless is said as a fact, you tend to wake right up. You argue clearly, and you usually know where to look things up.

It might be a debate about whether pandas are bears, or which year a song came out. Two minutes later you may have three facts and an article ready. And then someone might say quietly, “I don’t care who is right.”

The flip side is that you can win on facts and still miss the feeling. Try listening for what the talk is really about before you check anything.

When you make up, it is often after thinking it through. Facts can wait. People first.`},"4:balanced":{title:"You often ask for time to think before you answer.",gist:"You see both sides and like to think first, but the waiting can worry the other person.",body:`Your chart suggests a thoughtful way through disagreements. You tend to see both sides, sometimes more clearly than the people in them. You may not like to answer in a rush, and your answers tend to be careful and true.

Maybe you ask for a night to think, make tea and write two short lists: what hurt, and what you value about the other person. Or you take a long walk before you reply to a hard message.

One night can quietly become a week, though, while the other person waits in the silence. It can help to say when you will be back: “Give me until tomorrow evening.”

Take your time. Just let them know when it ends.`},"4:weak":{title:"A short reply can keep your mind busy for hours.",gist:"You notice small signs and can fill a silence with worry, then go quiet instead of asking.",body:`Small signs tend to reach you before words do. You feel things deeply, and when someone close goes cold, even for a minute, your mind may fill the silence with guesses. Few of them are kind to you.

It might be a one-word reply, “ok”, that you read again and again. Or a friend who seems quieter at lunch, and a whole afternoon of wondering why.

Starting a fight is rarely your way. You may go quiet and wait for the other person to notice, and they may not. Try asking, “Are we okay?” The answer is often simpler than the guesses.

You also tend to forgive quickly. Let asking be just as quick.`}},t={"strong:0":{title:"New beginnings wake you up, often several at once.",gist:"Fresh starts fill you with energy, though they can pile up and keep you going when tired.",body:`Wood is the element your chart holds most, and Saju links Wood with growth and starting things. In a strong chart, that growth tends to push outward in many directions at once. Beginnings can charge you more than almost anything else.

It might look like a free morning that turns into three plans: a new route to walk, a skill to try, a trip to think about. Or it might be the first week of anything new, when you feel most awake.

The flip side is that starts can pile up faster than you can care for them, and you may keep pushing when you are already tired. If you notice many half-grown projects, choose one to grow this month.

Roots grow on quiet days, too.`},"strong:1":{title:"People, laughter and being heard tend to light you up.",gist:"People and expression give you energy, though it can drop suddenly after a big week.",body:`Your chart holds more Fire than any other element. Fire is linked with warmth, expression and being seen, and in a strong chart it tends to burn bright. Your energy often goes outward: into stories, plans and people.

It might be the moment a quiet table starts laughing at a story you told. Or the end of a long, happy evening, when others are checking the time and you could easily keep going.

The flip side is that fire burns fast. After a big week, your energy may drop all at once, and quiet time can feel strange. If you notice that pattern, plan slow evenings the way you plan the busy ones.

A low flame still gives warmth.`},"strong:2":{title:"Like solid ground, your energy tends to hold for a long time.",gist:"Your energy starts slowly and then lasts, so you may notice your limits only after passing them.",body:`Of the five elements, Earth is the one your chart holds most. Saju links Earth with steadiness, care and solid ground. In a strong chart, that often shows as energy that is slow to start and then hard to stop.

It can look like helping a friend move and still carrying boxes when everyone else has sat down. Or a long day of being useful, when you only feel tired at the moment you finally stop.

The flip side is that your limits can arrive without warning. You may carry other people’s weight too, and asking for help can come late. If you notice that, take a short rest before you need it, not after.

Even a mountain is allowed to sit down.`},"strong:3":{title:"A clear goal tends to make you calm, fast and focused.",gist:"Clear goals sharpen your focus, though you can be strict with yourself and impatient with others.",body:`Metal leads in your chart: there is more of it than of any other element. Metal is linked with clarity, precision and finishing. In a strong chart, it often gives a sharp, focused energy that enjoys a hard problem.

It might show when a deadline gets close and you grow calmer, not louder: one list, one task at a time. Or in the quiet pleasure of something finished, like a sorted folder or a fix that finally works.

The flip side is that you can push too hard. You may be strict with yourself, and short with people who move more slowly. If you notice that, plan your rest as carefully as your work.

Rest is part of the work, too.`},"strong:4":{title:"Ideas tend to come in waves, sometimes when you want to sleep.",gist:"Ideas and learning fill you up, though your mind can keep running when you want rest.",body:`Your chart holds Water more than any other element, and Saju links Water with ideas, depth and flow. In a strong chart, that flow can be powerful. Your energy often comes in waves: a big rush of ideas, then a calm.

It might be the moment the lights go off and a handful of plans arrive at once. Or a long talk that jumps from films to history to a trip you might take one day.

The flip side is that the river may not stop when you want it to. Rest can be hard, and you may spread yourself over many paths. If you notice that, write the ideas down and choose one to follow.

One idea, followed all the way, can go further than ten.`},"balanced:0":{title:"Small, steady steps tend to take you further than big leaps.",gist:"You grow a little at a time, though you may wait too long to start.",body:`Wood is the element your chart holds most. Saju links it with growth and learning, and in a balanced chart that growth tends to be steady rather than wild. You often grow the way a plant does: a little, often.

It might be a skill that got better over a year of short practice, or a friendship that grew from small, regular talks. Progress you can see, even slow progress, tends to fill you up.

The flip side is that steady can become slow. You may wait for the perfect moment to start the next thing. If you notice that waiting, start anyway, with ten minutes a day.

A little, often, is still growth.`},"balanced:1":{title:"Your warmth is steady, and light and company make it brighter.",gist:"Light, music and good company warm you, though on grey days your spark can fade.",body:`Your chart holds more Fire than any other element, and Fire is linked with warmth and expression. In a balanced chart, that warmth tends to be steady: you can enjoy people and still enjoy coming home.

It might be a sunny morning that makes you open every window and put music on. Or a friend’s good news that you celebrate as if it were your own. On days like that, people can feel your warmth.

The flip side is that the spark can fade when the days are grey and all alike, and it may seem to wait for someone else to light it. If you notice that, be the one who starts it.

Put the music on first.`},"balanced:2":{title:"A regular rhythm tends to keep your energy calm and even.",gist:"Routine and familiar faces keep your energy even, though comfort can slowly turn into drift.",body:`Earth is the element your chart holds most, and Saju links Earth with stability, routine and trust. In a balanced chart, that tends to give an even, reliable energy. You may not burn bright and then go out; you tend to keep going.

It might be a favourite café where they start your order when you walk in, or a weekly meal with the same few people. Familiar faces and promises kept can make a day feel safe.

The flip side is that comfort can turn into drift. Weeks may go by in the same shape, and things you wanted to try can wait. If you notice that, keep your rhythm and put one small new thing inside it.

A routine can hold surprises too.`},"balanced:3":{title:"For you, a little order often feels like a kind of rest.",gist:"A clear plan helps you rest, though with big choices you may keep checking instead of deciding.",body:`Your chart holds more Metal than any other element. Metal is linked with order, clarity and finishing, and in a balanced chart it tends to give a clear, even energy. When a plan needs to change, you can often change it calmly.

It might look like packing the night before a trip, with a list, and sleeping well. Or clearing the desk before you start, and finding the work easier to see once the space is clear.

The flip side is that you may wait too long to feel certain. With big choices, checking one more time can feel safer than deciding. If you notice that, set a day to choose, and trust the list you made.

Good enough is often good.`},"balanced:4":{title:"You tend to follow your curiosity wherever the day goes.",gist:"You adapt easily and follow your curiosity, though weeks can pass without a direction you chose.",body:`Water leads in your chart, and Saju links Water with ideas, curiosity and flow. In a balanced chart, that flow tends to be easy. Your energy can move like a river, adapting to whatever the day brings.

It might be a free afternoon with no plan, where one side street leads to a bookshop and the bookshop leads to a park. Or a conversation that wanders somewhere better than where it started.

The flip side is that flowing can become drifting. Weeks can pass pleasantly without going anywhere you chose. If you notice that, pick one direction for the week and let the rest stay loose.

Let the day lead sometimes. On other days, choose where the river goes.`},"weak:0":{title:"You tend to grow best when someone grows beside you.",gist:"Encouragement helps you grow, though other people’s low moods can pull you down just as fast.",body:`Your chart holds more Wood than any other element, and Wood is linked with growth and new plans. In a gentle chart, that growth tends to reach toward good people, the way a young plant reaches toward light.

It might be a class that is easier to keep going to when a friend goes too. Or a plan that only starts to feel real once you say it out loud to someone who says, “Yes, do it.”

The flip side is that low moods can reach you just as easily as good ones. On your own, you may doubt a plan before it even starts. If you notice that, share the plan early with someone who believes in it.

Choose people who help you grow, then let them help.`},"weak:1":{title:"Kindness around you tends to make your own warmth shine.",gist:"You shine among kind people, though one cold look can make you doubt your own warmth.",body:`Fire is the element your chart holds most. Saju links it with warmth and being seen, and in a gentle chart that warmth tends to shine brightest in a warm room. You often notice everyone’s mood, and share your light easily.

It might be one kind word in the morning that still makes you smile in the afternoon. Or, on another day, one cold look that stays with you until evening.

The flip side is that you can take on the temperature of the room. One unkind moment can dim you, and you may wonder if your light is enough. If you notice that, stay near people who welcome your warmth, and keep some for yourself.

Your light is enough.`},"weak:2":{title:"Steady days and familiar people tend to keep you calm.",gist:"Familiar habits and people calm you, though you may carry the worries of those who lean on you.",body:`Your chart holds more Earth than any other element, and Earth is linked with care and steady ground. In a gentle chart, your energy tends to be steady when your days are steady. Like good soil, you often hold things together quietly.

It might be a weekly call with the same friend, or the same breakfast in a home that feels safe. When one of those small things changes, the whole week can feel a little off.

The flip side is that people lean on you, and their worries can join your own. When life gets loud, you may doubt your place in it. If you notice that, ask someone steady to hold some of it with you.

Protect your small rituals. They matter.`},"weak:3":{title:"Calm, orderly places tend to help you think clearly.",gist:"Calm, honest places help you think, though you can judge yourself harder than anyone else does.",body:`Metal is the element your chart holds most, and Saju links Metal with clarity and precision. In a gentle chart, that clarity tends to work best in calm, honest places. Noise and conflict can tire you faster than hard work does.

It might be a messy table that makes your thoughts messy too, until you clear one corner and the next step appears. Or a friend who says what they mean, and the relief of it.

The flip side is that a sharp eye can turn inward. You may be your own strictest judge, and doubt choices you made well. If you notice that, look at your own work the way you would look at a friend’s.

Be as kind to yourself as you are careful with your work.`},"weak:4":{title:"You tend to take in the moods of the people around you.",gist:"You can feel others’ moods as your own, so on busy days your own feelings can get lost.",body:`Your chart holds Water more than any other element, and Water is linked with intuition, depth and flow. In a gentle chart, it tends to work like water itself: it takes the shape of whatever holds it.

It might be needing an hour of quiet after a crowded day, before you answer a single message. Or a full train that leaves you tired in a way that has nothing to do with your legs.

The flip side is that you may carry home feelings that belong to other people. In a busy week, your own can get lost among them. If you notice that, sit quietly for a moment and sort them gently.

Before you carry a feeling home, ask, “Is this mine?”`}},n=[{title:"Growth and fresh starts can reach you by many roads.",gist:"Many charts have no Wood, and it is no flaw; tiny first steps help if starting feels slow.",body:`Saju links Wood with growth, plans and starting things: the green, reaching part of life. None of the characters in your chart is Wood. That is common, and it is not a flaw. About two charts in three have no character of at least one element.

The absence alone does not mean you find starting hard. Other parts of your chart, the years you live through and the people around you can all cover the same ground.

If you do recognise a habit of planning more than acting, try one small action a day, so small it feels almost silly: a first page, a first message, a first try. Caring for a plant could be a daily reminder of that kind of slow growth.

Start small. It still counts.`},{title:"Your warmth does not depend on how much Fire your chart holds.",gist:"No Fire in a chart is common, and it says nothing about how warm or open you are.",body:`Fire stands for warmth, expression and being seen: the part of life that shows what you feel. No character in your chart is Fire. Plenty of charts have none, and that says nothing bad about you.

It does not mean you are cold, or that your warmth goes unseen. Warmth can come through other parts of your chart and of your life: the people you love, the things you make, the way you listen.

If you recognise that your happiness tends to stay inside, try saying one warm thing out loud each day. It might be a thank-you, a compliment, a “that made my day.” A candle on the table could be a reminder to let some of it show.

However softly it shows, your warmth is real.`},{title:"Steadiness can grow from habits, whatever your chart holds.",gist:"A chart with no Earth is very ordinary, and steadiness can still grow from habits and people.",body:`Earth is linked with stability, care and routine: the ground under a day. Your chart shows no Earth. This is one of the most ordinary things a chart can show, and it is nothing to fix.

On its own, it does not make a life unsteady. Steadiness can come from other parts of your chart, from the people near you and from habits you build over the years.

If you recognise days that change shape so often that nothing stays, choose one small fixed point and keep it for a month: the same first song each morning, or a cup of something warm at the same time each afternoon. A note by the door could be the reminder.

Small anchors can make freedom feel safer.`},{title:"Choosing clearly is a skill, and skills can be practised.",gist:"Having no Metal does not make choosing hard, and small, kind limits can make it lighter.",body:`Metal is the element of structure, decisions and finishing: the clean edge that says “this, not that.” Your chart holds no Metal at all. An empty place in the chart is not an empty place in you.

Having no Metal is common, and it does not mean choosing is hard for you. Other parts of your chart, and everything you learn along the way, can give the same clear edge.

If you notice that small choices take you a long time, try a gentle limit: five minutes to decide where to eat, or one task finished before the next begins. Clearing out one drawer could be a small reminder that endings make room.

Each small choice can make the next one easier.`},{title:"Stillness and new ideas can find their way to you.",gist:"Zero Water is not a judgement on you; rest and new ideas can come in many ways.",body:`Water is linked with rest, ideas and flow: the part of life that goes around a rock instead of pushing it. Your chart has no Water character. That is a common count, not a verdict on who you are.

It does not mean you cannot rest, or that new ideas pass you by. Depth and calm can come through other parts of your chart, the seasons of a life and the things you love doing.

If you recognise that slow days feel strange, or that last-minute changes unsettle you, try one unplanned hour a week: a walk with no goal, or music with your eyes closed. The sound of rain could be a reminder to let a thought wander.

Stillness is not empty. Ideas often wait there.`}],a=[{title:"You often meet people as equals, whatever their age.",gist:"You stand on your own and meet people as equals, though leaning on others can feel hard.",body:`You tend to stand on your own and treat people as equals, whether they are older, younger or in charge. This part of your chart is traditionally read for peers, and for being trusted early.

You may recognise a home where you were treated as a grown-up early: maybe you had your own key, or your opinion was asked at the table. Maybe there were other children close in age, or friends who felt like family. If your story is different, the theme may still show up in how easily you look after yourself.

The flip side is that asking for help can feel strange. Try handing one small task to someone else this week.

Standing on your own is a strength. So is leaning, now and then.`},{title:"Speaking up for your share tends to come naturally to you.",gist:"You stand up for yourself easily, though you may compete even when there is plenty to share.",body:`You tend to speak up, argue your case and laugh with someone soon after a disagreement. This theme is linked with sharing and with friendly competition.

This often shows up as a lively home with many voices, where the remote control, the bathroom mirror or the last dumpling went to whoever asked first. Or the same lesson may have come from a crowded classroom, a team or a loud group of friends.

The flip side is that you may reach for your share even when there is plenty for everyone, and a slow, quiet meal can feel unusual. It can help to let someone else go first now and then, and notice how that feels.

There is room for you. You do not have to rush.`},{title:"You often know what makes a room feel good.",gist:"You have a gift for comfort and enjoyment, though hard talks may wait behind a kind gesture.",body:`Comfort is one of your gifts: you can often tell what to offer, when to put music on, and when a bad day needs something warm. Tradition links this theme with food, warmth and enjoying life.

Looking back, you may recognise a home where enjoying things mattered. Maybe it was soup on a cold evening, songs while someone cooked, or slow weekend breakfasts. Or the comfort may have come from somewhere else, like a friend’s family or a favourite café. The gift can grow either way.

Sometimes comfort takes the place of words. It might feel easier to make someone tea than to say what is wrong. When something needs saying, say it gently first, then make the tea.

Keep the warmth. Add the words.`},{title:"You tend to ask “why” before you follow a rule.",gist:"You think for yourself and speak up about unfairness, though you may push back before you listen.",body:`This theme is linked with questioning rules and finding your own voice. You tend to think for yourself, and when something seems unfair or makes no sense, you are often the first to say so.

It might go back to a home whose rules did not quite fit you: a bedtime, a dress code, or a rule nobody could quite explain. Or you may have found your voice somewhere else, at a strict school or in a club with old habits.

The flip side is that pushing back can come before listening, even when a rule is a good one. Try asking one honest question first, then decide.

Your voice was worth finding. Used calmly, it carries further.`},{title:"Last-minute changes of plan seldom unsettle you for long.",gist:"You are generous and adapt fast to change, though calm stretches may feel too quiet.",body:`Your chart points to generosity and an easy way with change. You tend to give freely to people, adapt quickly and stay steady when plans turn around. Traditionally, this theme is read for a busy, generous and changeable home life.

Maybe it was a home with people coming and going: guests who arrived without warning, a trip decided at breakfast, or someone with a big personality and bigger plans. Or you may see it now, in how quickly you make a stranger feel welcome.

Calm stretches, on the other hand, can feel too quiet, and good times may feel like something to enjoy fast or hold tightly. One small habit: keep one steady routine that is only yours.

Keep the open door. Steady ground can sit right beside it.`},{title:"Steady routines and kept promises tend to calm you.",gist:"You plan well and keep your word, though small treats for yourself can feel hard to allow.",body:`You tend to plan ahead, keep your promises and finish what you start. A clear routine often calms you, and people learn that they can rely on you. It is the theme of care, practical sense and steady days.

For some, it looks like a careful, practical home: dinner at the same time each day, a calendar on the wall that knew everyone’s plans, or leftovers that came back as lunch. Others build that steadiness later, with their own lists and habits.

The flip side is that sudden changes can unsettle you, and fun can start to feel like something to earn first. Try planning one small pleasure the way you plan everything else, and keeping it.

Being careful is a gift. Enjoying things can be part of the plan.`},{title:"Pressure and high standards can bring out your strength.",gist:"You handle pressure and high standards well, though rest and praise can be hard to accept.",body:`You tend to cope well with hard work, criticism and deadlines, and you rarely give up halfway. Traditionally, this theme is read for high standards and pressure that builds strength.

You may recognise a home with high hopes for you, where one good result often led to the next goal. Or the pressure may have come from elsewhere: a demanding school, a sport, or a time when you simply had to manage. However it came, it can leave a toughness others lean on.

The cost can be an inner voice that is hard to satisfy. Rest may feel like falling behind, and praise may be hard to believe. Try writing down one thing you did well each day, and leaving it there.

You do not have to earn rest.`},{title:"Doing things properly often matters a lot to you.",gist:"Your manners and sense of duty earn trust quickly, though small mistakes can feel huge.",body:`You may have a strong sense of how things should be done. You tend to keep your word and take responsibility seriously, so people often trust you quickly. Structure, manners and a good name belong to this theme.

It might look like a home where doing things properly mattered: shoes lined up by the door, a proper greeting for every guest, a thank-you after every gift. Or you may have learned these habits elsewhere, from a teacher, a coach or a team.

The flip side is a feeling that you must always be correct. Small mistakes can feel much bigger than they are. With people you trust, a little mess can be allowed to stay.

Good manners are a gift to others. Ease is one you can give yourself.`},{title:"You can be quietly different, and at ease on your own.",gist:"You are independent and see what others miss, though you may keep hurts to yourself.",body:`You tend to find your own way of seeing things. You do not need a crowd to tell you who you are, and you often notice what others miss. Tradition reads this theme as independence and a quiet kind of difference.

It can point to a home that was not quite like other homes, with its own food, beliefs or hours, or to long afternoons spent reading, drawing or inventing worlds. Tradition also links it with a bond at home that felt close in some ways and far in others. If that fits, no one is to blame. If not, leave it aside.

One thing to notice: you may keep hurts to yourself. Telling one trusted person one small thing can be a good start.

Different was never wrong.`},{title:"You often trust that help exists when you need it.",gist:"You learn well and accept care easily, though you may wait for approval before deciding.",body:`You often love learning, take good advice well and let people look after you, which is rarer than it sounds. This theme is linked with care, support and books.

You may recognise a home with one steady person at its centre. Maybe someone left a light on until you got home, kept books on the shelf or helped with homework. It might have been a parent, a grandparent, a teacher or a neighbour. If your story is different, the theme can still show up in how warmly you accept help now.

The flip side is that you may wait for someone’s approval before you decide. Try one small choice alone, and let it stand.

You know how to receive care. You can give it to yourself, too.`}],s={"none:0":{title:"Time alone tends to feel like good company to you.",gist:"You enjoy your own company, and friends may not always know how much they matter to you.",body:`Your own company tends to feel easy and good. You tend to choose your people one by one, and friendship for you is often about room: room to be yourself, and room for the other person too.

It might be a film you see alone, on purpose, and enjoy all the more. Or a friendship where months pass without a call, and then it feels as if no time has passed at all.

The flip side is that people may not know how much they matter to you. If you rarely send the first message, try sending one, for no reason at all.

Your quiet way of caring is real. A short message lets people see it.`},"none:1":{title:"The people who get your stranger jokes tend to stay close.",gist:"You connect through shared humour and ideas, but you may lean on one person for everything.",body:`A shared sense of humour is often where your friendships start. A crowd can feel less like home to you than one person who really gets you.

It might be a joke at a big table that only one person laughs at, very hard. Or a talk about a strange dream or a favourite song that goes on until the café closes.

What can happen is leaning on one person for everything, or keeping your odd side hidden in other groups. It can help to share one small odd thing with someone new, and see who laughs.

You do not need a crowd. One real laugh can be enough.`},"none:2":{title:"Friendship, for you, often looks like being useful.",gist:"You make friends through shared tasks and help, but may only call when there is a job.",body:`Your friendships tend to grow out of real life: a shared project, a problem solved together, a favour that turned into lunch. Often the people who stay are the ones who show up.

You may show care by being useful. Maybe you know a good repair shop, lend the right tool or fill in the form nobody understands. Or you find the cheaper ticket for everyone.

You might call only when there is something to do, and being helped yourself can feel strange. Try one call with no task at all, just to talk.

Being useful is one kind of love. Simply being there is another.`},"none:3":{title:"Promises and secrets tend to be safe with you.",gist:"You are a loyal, reliable friend, though some people may only ever see your polite side.",body:`Friendship is something you tend to take seriously. You keep promises and secrets, and people often trust you with what matters to them. You may choose your people slowly, and once you do, you tend to stay.

It might be arriving early to help someone carry boxes, or remembering a date they mentioned once. With the people you trust most, you may finally stop being the responsible one and just laugh.

The cost is that you can seem formal or hard to reach at first, and some people may only see your polite side. It can help to let one person see you when you are not in charge of anything.

Loyalty is your gift. Let some fun in with it.`},"none:4":{title:"Ideas and long talks are often how your friendships begin.",gist:"You bond through ideas and honest advice, but a long, careful reply can wait for weeks.",body:`Thinking together is one of the ways you connect best. You tend to pick people who think with you, and you give them deep attention and honest advice. Feeling understood may matter more to you than being busy together.

It might be a book passed back and forth with notes in the margins. Or a long voice message about an idea, answered days later with another one.

The flip side is living a little too much in your head. A long, perfect reply can sit in your drafts while the other person waits. Try sending a short message today; the long one can follow.

Depth is your way. A quick hello keeps it alive.`},"some:0":{title:"You tend to like friends who are equals and push back.",gist:"You enjoy friendship as equals taking turns, though you may keep your own problems to yourself.",body:`Your chart points to friendship between equals. You tend to like people who laugh with you, push back and take turns: you host, they host; you choose, they choose. A little friendly rivalry can keep things lively.

It might be a game night with loud arguments about the rules, or a running contest over who walks the most steps in a month. Maybe a small, silly prize that changes hands once a year.

One thing to notice: you may be so used to being strong among equals that you keep your own problems to yourself. Try telling one friend about something hard, before it is solved.

Taking turns works for help too.`},"some:1":{title:"You often bring the stories, the plans and the playlist.",gist:"You bring energy and fun to your friends, but few may think to ask how you are.",body:`Around people you like, you often bring the energy and the ideas. You enjoy people who enjoy you as you are, and you can take honest feedback from them when you ask for it.

Maybe there is a story people ask you to tell again, even though they know the ending. Or you are the one who suggests karaoke, a picnic or a new place to try.

The cost can be becoming the fun one, always on. People may forget to ask how you really are, and you may forget to say. It can help to let a close friend see the quiet you now and then.

The quiet you is good company too.`},"some:2":{title:"The small details of a plan often end up with you.",gist:"You keep things fair and practical among friends, but may quietly count who gives what.",body:`Among friends, you are often the practical one, and fairness matters to you. Give and take tends to feel natural: you sort out the details, others bring their own strengths, and things run smoothly.

It might be the moment a bill arrives and faces turn to you for the split. Or a shared list for a trip, with times and addresses, that you started. You may also notice quietly when someone could use a little extra help.

The flip side is becoming the group’s manager, or keeping a silent count of who gives what. One small habit: let someone else hold the plan once in a while.

Fair is good. Easy is good too.`},"some:3":{title:"You tend to turn “we should meet up” into a date.",gist:"You turn talk into real plans, but it can hurt when nobody else ever organises.",body:`You are often the one who makes plans real. You keep promises, remember dates and follow through, and people tend to respect you for it. With people you trust, you may let yourself relax once the plan is made.

Maybe a group has said “we should meet up soon” for weeks, and you are the one who suggests three dates and a place. Or you remember a birthday that everyone else is about to miss.

You may notice that you are usually the organiser, and that it hurts when nobody else steps in. Try letting someone else plan the next one, even if it is a little messy.

A messy plan can still make a good day.`},"some:4":{title:"People often come to you for slow, careful advice.",gist:"Friends tend to trust your careful advice, but you may give it more easily than you take it.",body:`Small things others miss often reach you first, and you tend to think before you speak. People often trust your advice for that. Help tends to go both ways around you: you listen, and you are looked after too.

It might be a screenshot of a confusing message, sent with one word: “Thoughts?” You might read slowly and say what you see. Or someone may tell you, kindly, to stop thinking, eat something and sleep.

Advice may flow out of you far more easily than it flows in. Try asking a friend what they think about something of yours, and really listen.

You are allowed to be the one asking.`},"many:0":{title:"Everyone tends to be an equal in your eyes, and it shows.",gist:"You share your time freely and treat people as equals, so your own plans can come last.",body:`Your chart suggests that friendship takes up a lot of room in your life. You tend to treat everyone as an equal, and people often feel that. You share time easily, and a bit of friendly competition can keep you sharp.

It might be a quick errand that takes an hour, because you keep stopping to talk. Or a weekend that fills up with other people’s plans before you have made your own.

Everyone may get a piece of your time, and your own plans can come last. Try keeping a few hours each week that belong only to you.

The people who matter will understand.`},"many:1":{title:"Small plans around you tend to grow into big ones.",gist:"Your energy draws people together, but you can give so much that little is left for you.",body:`People tend to gather where you are. Your stories, food or music can turn a small plan into a lively one, and you share freely. Someone may want the same spotlight, too, and that can keep things interesting.

Maybe a dinner for four ends with people sitting on the floor. Or a short walk becomes an afternoon with friends of friends, and someone has brought a guitar.

The cost can be giving the crowd so much that little is left for you, and some friendships may never go deeper than the party. It can help to keep one quiet evening for one or two people.

The day after the party matters too.`},"many:2":{title:"You often know someone who can help with almost anything.",gist:"You connect people and share favours freely, but money and friendship can get mixed.",body:`You tend to be a bridge between people. Chances, tips and favours move around you easily, and you pass them on. You may pay first, lend what you have and trust it to come back.

It might be a friend who needs a mover, a kind dentist or a cake by Saturday, and two messages from you that solve it before lunch. Or a small project that starts with a friend and grows into something shared.

The flip side is that money and friendship can get mixed, and it can be hard to tell who comes for you and who comes for the favours. Notice who calls when they need nothing.

Keep those people close.`},"many:3":{title:"You may join a group for fun and end up leading it.",gist:"People tend to trust you to hold a group together, and friendship can turn into duty.",body:`Groups often hold together around you. People trust you, and you take on responsibility easily, so roles tend to find their way to you. In a busy circle, someone may want that role too.

It might be a book club, a choir or a language class where, after a few months, you hold the keys and run the group chat. Or a trip where everyone’s tickets are somehow on your phone.

Over time, friendship can slowly turn into duty, and you may forget how to simply belong. Try coming to the next meeting as a member, not the leader.

You are allowed to just enjoy it.`},"many:4":{title:"You tend to learn something from almost everyone you meet.",gist:"You learn from the people around you, but many voices can make your own hard to hear.",body:`Curiosity about people is one of your strengths, and people often enjoy teaching you. Friendship can be a kind of school for you: a new word, a new dish, a new way of seeing something. In return you often give attention, long talks and good advice.

Maybe you say thank you in a language a friend taught you. Or someone might check on you when you go quiet, before you have said a thing.

So many opinions can fill your head, though, that your own gets hard to hear. Try keeping one evening a week just for your own thoughts.

Your own voice deserves room too.`}},i=[{title:"You tend to settle in by finding the people first.",gist:"You tend to settle in through people and readiness, though always being ready can make rest hard.",body:`A place tends to feel like home to you when life is close and you feel ready for anything. Somewhere new, people often matter to you before rooms do.

In a new area, you might find the nearest station, a shop that stays open late and a friend nearby before anything else. Maybe there is a drawer with snacks and a spare charger, just in case.

The flip side is that a home full of plans can be hard to rest in. When you are away, you may miss voices more than places. Try one evening a week with nothing to prepare for.

Being ready is a skill. So is being still.`},{title:"You tend to put down deep roots wherever you stay.",gist:"Familiar places tend to steady you, so changing places can feel slow and hard.",body:`Slow, steady places tend to suit you: a quiet street, a solid door, a daily rhythm that is yours. Once you settle somewhere, the place can become part of you.

Maybe it is the same bakery for years, where you notice on the first bite that the bread has changed. Or a walk you know so well that your feet choose the turns. In a new place, the first months may feel wrong before they feel right.

The other side is that a change of place can feel like being pulled out of the ground, and you may stay somewhere after it stops fitting you. Once a year, it can help to ask whether it still does.

Roots are a gift, and they can grow in new ground too.`},{title:"You tend to need light, air and room to breathe.",gist:"Open, bright spaces tend to suit you, though quick choices can bring surprises.",body:`Open space tends to matter to you more than to most: light, air, room to move and nobody telling you what to do in your own space. Small, dark rooms may leave you restless, and you often decide with your heart, and fast.

It might be a window you open wide first thing every morning. Or maybe, looking at a room, one big window is enough for a yes in five minutes, even with a crack in the wall.

Quick choices can bring surprises, though, and a space you liked at once may start to feel small just as quickly. Waiting one more day before a big yes can help.

Follow the light. Give it one night’s sleep first.`},{title:"You tend to make a space yours with small, gentle touches.",gist:"You tend to make places calm and lovely, though you may live with problems too long.",body:`Softness and calm are often what make a place feel like home to you: warm light, a few chosen things, no shouting through the walls. Small touches are how you make a space your own.

Somewhere new, perhaps the lamp finds its corner before the spoons are found. Or even a room for one night gets a scarf over the chair and feels a little more like you. People may say your space feels peaceful.

What to notice: you may live with a problem around your home for months rather than raise it, like a noisy neighbour or a light that does not work. Try raising it early, in a calm, short message.

A peaceful home sometimes needs one clear sentence.`},{title:"You tend to talk about your area as if you built it.",gist:"You tend to want space for big plans, and may keep looking at the next, bigger place.",body:`Room to be big tends to matter to you: a long table, a wall for plans, space for the people you love. You often grow proud of wherever you live, and when a bigger chance appears, you may feel ready to go towards it.

Friends who visit might get a full tour of the best noodle place and the hidden park, whether they asked or not. Maybe within a month the people in the local shops know your name.

The harder part is that your eye may keep moving to the next, bigger place, and home can start to feel like a step rather than a rest. Try letting the place you have be enough for tonight.

Aim high, and rest where you are.`},{title:"You tend to know quickly whether a place feels right.",gist:"A private, quiet home tends to suit you, though you may keep people out and miss home silently.",body:`Privacy is often what makes a place feel like yours: soft light, a few good things chosen with care, and quiet that is only yours. You tend to trust your feeling about a place more than any list.

You might step into a room, look at the light on the floor and know within seconds. Maybe very few people have been inside your space, and that suits you.

The flip side is that you may keep people outside a little too long. And when you miss home, or a place you loved, you might tell no one. Telling one person when that feeling comes can help.

Guard your quiet. Let one or two people share it.`},{title:"Home tends to be your starting point more than your resting place.",gist:"You tend to love life outside your door, so home can get only what is left.",body:`Life outside the door tends to matter to you as much as the rooms inside: a busy street, a park, a town that stays awake. For you, home is often where the day starts rather than where it slows down.

Maybe the fridge holds one lemon and a jar of jam, and your plants have learned to live on very little. You may know every café nearby before you know the neighbours.

The other side is that home can get whatever is left of you. When you finally stop, your own room may feel strangely quiet. Try giving it one slow evening a week: cook something, sit down, stay.

Go out as much as you like. Home will still be there, ready for you.`},{title:"You tend to build a soft nest, one corner at a time.",gist:"Calm, gentle spaces tend to feel like home, and time away can bring worry and homesickness.",body:`A calm, gentle nest tends to suit you best: soft things, handmade things, maybe a window with some green outside. Noise and rushing can tire you, and you often settle in slowly, one cosy corner at a time.

It might be a cup that fits your hands just right, or a blanket someone made. Maybe, halfway down the street, you turn back to check that the stove is off. It was.

Time away can be hard, though. You may worry about home and miss it more than you expected. One soft thing that comes with you, a scarf or a photo, can be a small, steady reminder of it.

Your gentleness makes any corner softer.`},{title:"You tend to treat a room as a place to experiment.",gist:"You tend to settle in fast and keep changing things, though places can start to bore you.",body:`You tend to feel at home where your mind can play: something going on, new things to try, people to meet. You often settle in fast, because a new place has so much to explore.

Perhaps a desk slowly turns into a workshop, or the space gets a new idea every season: a reading corner, a tiny studio, a wall of hooks. Maybe a few half-finished projects wait in the corners.

Notice if a place starts to feel old before it really is. You may want a change of address more often than you need one. Try changing one small thing in the room first.

Keep playing with your space. Let a few things stay.`},{title:"You tend to feel calm when everything has its place.",gist:"Order and clear routines tend to settle you, though other people’s mess can bother you a lot.",body:`Order tends to be what makes a place restful for you. Clear routines, clean lines and a space you are glad to show can calm your mind.

This can look like jars with labels, or dishes done before you sit down. Maybe, if you ever move, the boxes are numbered and the new place feels like yours on the day the last one is empty.

Other people’s mess can bother you more than you would like, though. If you share a space, living with people often means living with their socks. Agreeing on a few shared rules can save a hundred small corrections.

Keep your order. Let one drawer be messy, just for fun.`},{title:"A place tends to feel like home once you trust it.",gist:"You tend to settle slowly because home means trust, and may miss an old place for years.",body:`Trust is what tends to turn a place into home for you: people you know, and places that know you. You often watch over your space and the people in it, and settling in somewhere new can take time.

It might be knowing the name of the dog on your street, or checking the lock twice before bed. Maybe, after a move, you still travel back to an old area for a haircut, because that person knows your story.

Loyalty to a place can last after the place stops suiting you, and an old home may stay in your heart for years. If you are somewhere new, try letting it earn a little trust each week.

Your loyalty can come with you wherever you go.`},{title:"You tend to make room for one more at your table.",gist:"You tend to fill a place with food and guests, leaving little quiet for yourself.",body:`Good food and good company tend to make any place warmer for you: happy voices, a full pot and room for one more. You may get to know a new place through taste first, finding the good street food or the market with the sweetest fruit.

A quick visit to your place might turn into dinner and end near midnight. Maybe everyone who visits knows where you keep the extra blankets.

The flip side is that home can become everyone’s place, with little quiet left for you. And when you are far away, the taste of home may be what you miss most. Try keeping one evening a week just for yourself.

Keep the door open, and save some warmth for you.`}],r={title:"Your feet tend to be restless, in the best way.",gist:"New places and changes tend to refresh you, though part of you may miss somewhere else.",body:`Your chart carries a restless, travelling energy. A change of scene tends to feel like fresh air, and you can often make a home almost anywhere. For you, home may be something you carry: a few things and a few people.

It might be a long trip or a new address. It might also be smaller: a new way home, a café across town, a day somewhere you have never been. Maybe you can pack a bag in ten minutes.

Part of you may often be somewhere else, though: here, you miss there; there, you miss here. A small ritual that goes with you, like the same morning song, can help each place feel like yours.

Go where you like. Leave a little of yourself in each place.`},h={"0:none":{title:"For you, feelings tend to grow slowly, one ring at a time.",gist:"You tend to build your own life first, and feelings can grow so slowly they look like friendship.",body:`You tend to grow on your own terms, with your own plans and at your own pace. Romance is rarely the centre of that picture. When feelings do grow, they grow the way a tree adds rings: slowly, quietly, with no announcement.

It might be a friend of many years, where trust came first. Or a neighbour who became easy to talk to, one ordinary week at a time. Maybe a friend has to point it out before you see it yourself.

The cost is that others may not read you. Steady interest can look like plain friendship from the outside. If someone matters to you, a few clear words can help.

A life that grows on its own is full too.`},"0:some":{title:"When you choose someone, you tend to choose for years.",gist:"You tend to know what you want in a partner, and you can be slow to bend.",body:`You often have a clear idea of who you could build a life beside: someone with values and a direction of their own. When you care for someone, it tends to last for years, and you stand up for them even when they are not there.

It can show in small ways. Maybe you remember a plan someone mentioned months ago and ask how it is going. Or you ask a new person about their goals sooner than most people would.

What can be hard is bending. In a disagreement you can stand very straight, and advice may arrive before anyone asked for it. Try listening to the end, then asking, “Do you want ideas, or should I just listen?”

Strong roots still let branches move.`},"0:many":{title:"Your kindness tends to reach many people, and some hope for more.",gist:"Your protective calm draws attention easily, and saying a clear no can be the hard part.",body:`Your chart points to a lot of attention coming your way. You tend to be steady, fair and protective, and people feel safe near that, the way they might stand under a big tree in the rain.

It can start with something small. Maybe you help someone carry something heavy up the stairs, explain a hard task, or stay late to help a friend finish. Later, one or two of them may hope for more than friendship.

Saying no can be the hard part. Your kindness reaches many people, and a polite yes can come out when you mean no. A clear, kind answer, given early, is often easier for both sides.

A tree gives shade to many, and still grows in one place.`},"1:none":{title:"Love tends to reach you sideways, through groups and shared plans.",gist:"You tend to get along with everyone and feel fine alone, so your own wishes can stay hidden.",body:`Adapting comes easily to you. You find common ground with almost anyone, and you are often perfectly happy on your own. When feelings grow, they tend to grow sideways, the way a vine does: through a group, a hobby or something you share.

It might be a team where one person slowly becomes your favourite to talk to. Or a group chat where you notice you answer one name a little faster than the rest.

Because you fit in so well, people may not know which way you would lean, and sometimes you may not know either. Now and then, say out loud what you would choose, even about something small.

You are allowed to lean towards something on purpose.`},"1:some":{title:"You tend to get close patiently, in many small ways.",gist:"You get close gently and patiently, though you may bend until someone else’s tastes replace yours.",body:`When you like someone, you tend to know it, and you do not give up easily. You rarely push. Instead you find small, patient ways closer, the way a vine finds its way up a wall.

It might look like reading a book someone mentioned once, then bringing up a line from it. Or learning the name of their favourite band. In a relationship, two lives can slowly grow into each other: shared jokes, shared plans, shared friends.

Something to watch is bending too far. Another person’s music, friends and plans can slowly take the place of yours. One small habit: keep one thing each week that is only yours.

Grow close, and keep your own leaves.`},"1:many":{title:"People often leave a talk with you feeling like your favourite.",gist:"Your soft charm makes each person feel special, and choosing one can take a long time.",body:`Your charm tends to be soft and real. You find the thing that makes each person light up, and you give it your full attention. Romance can find you easily, from more than one side.

Maybe you remember the name of someone’s cat from months ago. Or you laugh at a nervous person’s joke until they relax. By the end of an evening, a few people may feel there was something special between you.

Choosing may take you a long time. A vine can grow around almost anything nearby, and several doors may stay half open. A clear answer, even a gentle no, is a kindness too.

Choose what you grow towards.`},"2:none":{title:"You tend to shine on everyone, and romance can wait.",gist:"Your warmth reaches everyone, so if you do like someone, they may not see it.",body:`Your warmth tends to go everywhere: to friends, to neighbours, to the stranger at the next table. Romance is not the centre of your sky. When love does grow, it often grows out of friendship, once someone has seen your quiet days as well as your bright ones.

It might be karaoke, where you sing the love songs loudest and then sit down perfectly calm. Or a friend’s birthday, where you lead the cheering and go home happy on your own.

The cost is that people may not tell when you mean it. Deeper interest can look like your usual warmth. When it is real, one quiet sentence, said away from the crowd, says it best.

Your light is whole, with or without romance.`},"2:some":{title:"When you love, you tend to love out loud.",gist:"You love openly and generously, and a small response to a big gesture can hurt.",body:`When you care for someone, you rarely hide it. You tend to want a love that is warm, open and happy to be seen, with someone who shows it back.

It can look like a surprise dinner, a handwritten card on an ordinary day, or a cake with candles for a small win. Maybe you clap the loudest when someone you love does well.

You may also need to feel seen in return. If a big gesture meets a small reaction, your light can dim fast, and you may go quiet instead of saying so. Try putting the need into simple words. A hug can be asked for.

Big gestures are lovely. So are small, plain ones.`},"2:many":{title:"Warmth pours out of you, and attention tends to follow.",gist:"Romance starts easily for you, but when the first sparkle fades, staying can take effort.",body:`Warmth tends to pour out of you, so attention finds you in many places. People want to be near you, and beginnings come easily.

It might be a birthday where a few messages are a little warmer than “happy birthday”. Or a new club where, by the second week, someone always saves you a seat.

The challenge can come later, on an ordinary day with no surprises. When the first excitement cools, a new face may look brighter than a familiar one. If you are with someone, it can help to look for what ordinary days hold: a shared joke, a trust that grew slowly.

The sun matters because it comes back every morning.`},"3:none":{title:"You tend to read other hearts better than your own.",gist:"You understand other people’s feelings well, and your own can stay in the background.",body:`Feelings make sense to you, especially other people’s. Your own heart tends to move more slowly. When love grows for you, it often starts as a close friendship, a small light you keep low until you are sure.

It might be a friend who sends you a message they are nervous about, and you change one word before they press send. Or being the friend people call when they cannot stop thinking about someone.

Over time, you can become the helper in everyone else’s story and stay at the edge of your own. Give your own feelings the same patient attention you give theirs, even if you only write them down.

Your heart deserves the care you give so easily.`},"3:some":{title:"You tend to notice every small change in someone you love.",gist:"You give close, devoted attention, but a short silence can feel bigger than it is.",body:`Your chart suggests a close, devoted kind of love. You tend to know what you want, and when you care for someone, they get your full attention, like a small flame lighting one face in a dark room.

It can look like seeing that someone is tired before they say a word, or knowing which part of a story they will want to talk about first. Maybe at the cinema you watch their face as much as the screen.

The same gift has a cost. A quiet evening can start to feel like a message, and you may replay the day looking for what went wrong. When you are not sure, ask. It is kinder than guessing, for both of you.

Keep some of that attention for yourself.`},"3:many":{title:"After one long talk with you, people tend to feel understood.",gist:"Your warm attention draws many people, but it can tire you, and kindness may look like a promise.",body:`You tend to give people something rare: close, warm attention. You ask one real question and listen to the whole answer. Many people want to sit near that kind of light, so romance can come to you often.

It might be a chat on some steps after an event that somehow lasts an hour. Or a quiet corner at a gathering where someone ends up telling you about their whole year. The next day, a message may be waiting.

But one candle cannot light every room. If your glow goes to everyone, you may feel tired, and a kind evening can look like a promise. Being clear early about what you are offering helps everyone.

Choose the room you want to light.`},"4:none":{title:"You tend to help first and talk about feelings much later.",gist:"You give help easily and ask for little, so others may not find a way in.",body:`Romance is rarely a sudden spark for you. You tend to be steady on your own, and when love comes, it is usually built slowly, often from a friendship that has lasted through hard days.

It might look like being the first to arrive when a friend moves house, with tape and boxes. Or fixing a wobbly table at someone’s place without saying a word about it.

People may forget that someone so solid likes to be looked after too. If you notice you always give and rarely ask, try asking for one small thing. Letting someone help is a way of letting them closer.

If you want company on the mountain, leave a path open.`},"4:some":{title:"You tend to show love in actions more than in words.",gist:"Your love is steady and practical, but words come slowly and hurts can stay unspoken.",body:`What you want is often something solid and built to last, with someone who values being there as much as you do. Your love is rarely loud. It stays, like a mountain outside the window.

It can look like waiting at the station with a warm drink when someone’s train is late. Or noticing that their favourite snack is back in the shop and buying two.

Words may come more slowly, and someone close may want to hear love, not only see it. When something hurts, you may stay quiet until it feels too heavy to move. Saying one kind thing and one honest thing out loud each week can make a real difference.

Even a mountain has an echo.`},"4:many":{title:"People tend to rest easily near your calm.",gist:"Many people feel at ease near you, but you may let love happen instead of choosing it.",body:`Calm, patient and solid: people tend to relax around that. Many want to be near your calm, and some start to hope for more than friendship.

Maybe on a long bus ride someone falls asleep on your shoulder, and you do not move for an hour. Or a friend keeps finding a reason to sit next to you at every group outing.

Deciding can be harder. You may be slow to move, so things can happen around you rather than by your choice. Someone settles in, and staying is easy. Now and then, ask yourself plainly: is this what I want?

Calm is a gift. So is choosing.`},"5:none":{title:"You tend to love by looking after people, quietly.",gist:"Love tends to grow from long, quiet care, and letting others care for you can feel harder.",body:`Care, for you, tends to be practical and quiet, and romance is often not the first thing on your mind. When love grows, it can grow like a garden: from care given over a long time, until something is there that nobody planted on purpose.

It might be holding a spare key for a friend, just in case. Or turning up with soup in someone’s hard week, and washing the dishes before you leave.

You may look after everyone and forget to ask what you need. Try letting someone return a favour, even a small one. Being cared for can feel strange at first, and that is fine.

Let someone bring the soup to you.`},"5:some":{title:"Your care tends to show in the small, practical things.",gist:"You care in steady, practical ways, though worry can grow and your own needs stay quiet.",body:`Your chart points to a steady, practical kind of love, the kind a life can be built on. You tend to know what you want, and you show care through details, so the people you love have room to grow.

It might be remembering which days are long for someone and having dinner ready. Or carrying a spare umbrella that is not really for you.

Worry can be the other side. You may plan for problems before they exist, and keep your own needs quiet until you feel tired and unseen. It can help to say one need out loud, as plainly as you would offer help.

Good soil needs rain too.`},"5:many":{title:"Your care tends to make people feel at home.",gist:"Your care draws grateful people close, and being needed can feel a lot like being loved.",body:`People often feel at home near you. You tend to be patient, practical and kind, and attention often reaches you through gratitude, which can turn into romance quickly.

Maybe a friend arrives with a cracked phone and leaves with a plan for fixing it. Or someone has a hard month, and you are the one who checks in every few days. Some of them come back, and a few want to stay.

Choosing is where it can get hard. You may be drawn to the people who need you most. Being needed can feel like being loved, but it is not always the same. One question can help: does this person also help me grow?

You can be needed and looked after, both.`},"6:none":{title:"You tend to prefer plain words, and may miss soft hints.",gist:"You value plain words and respect, so quieter signs of interest can slip past you.",body:`Loyal, direct and independent, you tend to treat people as equals first, and you prefer plain words to games. When love grows, it often grows out of respect: someone who argued with you fairly, worked beside you, or stood with you on a hard day.

It might be someone who asked about your weekend every Monday, and you thought they were just being polite. Or a friend who mentions, much later, that someone liked you.

Your own goals can keep you busy, and soft signals may not reach you. If something feels unclear, you can ask in your own plain way: “Was that an invitation?”

Not everyone speaks as plainly as you, and that is fine too.`},"6:some":{title:"In love, you tend to say what you mean.",gist:"You are direct and loyal in love, but plain honesty can hurt more than you intend.",body:`Games rarely interest you in love. You tend to know who you want, and once you choose someone, you stand by them firmly. Nobody has to guess what you feel.

It might be asking someone out in one clear sentence, then waiting calmly for the answer. Or telling someone the truth about their cooking, kindly but completely.

Your honesty can cut, though. A true sentence is not always what the moment needs, and in an argument you may win the point and lose the evening. Before you answer, try asking: is this true, and is it helpful right now?

Keep your words straight, and their edges soft.`},"6:many":{title:"You tend to know quickly, and people find that refreshing.",gist:"Your directness draws many people, but you may end things before trying to mend them.",body:`Your courage and directness tend to draw people in. You rarely keep anyone guessing, and many people find that refreshing. Invitations can come often, and you are not afraid to say yes.

It might be a first coffee where you know within ten minutes, and say so kindly either way. Or a dance class where you are the one who asks first.

Staying can be the harder part. When something turns dull or difficult, you may decide quickly that it is over, before trying to fix it. When you feel that pull, wait a day and have one honest talk first.

A good blade is shaped slowly, in the heat.`},"7:none":{title:"You tend to be selective, and happy in your own company.",gist:"You choose slowly and enjoy time alone, so a good person may wait long at your door.",body:`Your own taste tends to be clear, and you do not give your heart to just anyone. Time alone rarely feels lonely to you. When love grows, it tends to grow slowly, from a friendship where someone shows in many small ways that they truly see you.

It might be a free afternoon at a gallery, reading every label, with one small, perfect pastry after. Or a film you picked entirely for yourself, watched alone and enjoyed fully.

Your door can stay closed a long time, and a small flaw may catch your eye early. Try giving one good person a second look before you decide.

Being happy alone means you can choose freely.`},"7:some":{title:"You tend to love with precision: the right word, the right moment.",gist:"You love with care and high standards, but a careless word can stay with you a long time.",body:`In love, you often know exactly what you want, and when you find it, you are deeply devoted. Your love shows in the details, chosen with great care.

It might be weeks spent choosing a small present that fits in one hand. Or a short note with a date on it that only two people understand.

You may also be easily hurt, and you remember. A careless joke can stay with you for months, and high standards can turn into a quiet list of small disappointments. Try naming a hurt gently within a day or two, before the list grows.

Not every scratch is a crack.`},"7:many":{title:"You tend to be noticed, and you tend to notice flaws.",gist:"Attention comes to you easily, but a sharp eye for flaws can make choosing and staying hard.",body:`A quiet shine tends to follow you, and people see it. Attention can come easily: compliments, invitations, people curious about what is behind your calm face.

It might be a wedding where more than one person asks for your number. Or a new group where one person keeps asking you questions. On the way home, you may already have noticed each person’s small flaws.

The hard part can be choosing, and then staying. With many options, it is easy to keep looking for someone perfect. When a small flaw appears, set it next to three things you like, and see which side is heavier.

Nothing real is perfect. Real can be better.`},"8:none":{title:"Freedom and big ideas tend to matter more to you than romance.",gist:"You value space and deep talks, so close friends may not know where they stand.",body:`Your chart suggests a free, wide-open kind of heart. When love comes, it often comes through other doors: a long friendship, a shared adventure, a mind that keeps up with yours. You may be slow to give it a name.

It might be a friend in another time zone, and three-hour talks about whether people can change. Or a friend from a trip long ago, someone you still write to.

Something to notice is drifting. If everything stays open, people close to you may not know what they are to you. It can help to tell them once, in plain words.

You can name something and still be free.`},"8:some":{title:"You tend to love deeply and still need room to wander.",gist:"You value depth and room in love, though your long silences can feel like distance to others.",body:`Depth and room tend to matter to you in love: someone with a deep world of their own, who lets you wander in your thoughts and is still there when you come back. Your love can be wide and deep, full of ideas you bring back from far away.

It might be an evening by a river where you go quiet for ten minutes, then ask, “What if we lived somewhere new for a year?” Or a long drive where the silence feels like company.

From the outside, silence can look like distance. The person beside you cannot see where your mind went. A short sentence can help: “I am thinking, not leaving.”

Share the map now and then.`},"8:many":{title:"Wherever you go, people tend to want you to stay longer.",gist:"Your depth draws people from many sides, but you may move on before anything has time to grow.",body:`Deep, curious and a little mysterious: people tend to want to know what is underneath. Attention can come to you from many sides, the way rivers run to the sea.

It might be a summer course, a new neighbourhood or a short trip, where you arrive with no plan and leave with new friends. Maybe someone asks, at the end, “Do you really have to go?”

What can be hard is staying. Every new shore looks interesting, and you may move on before anything has time to grow. Before you leave something, try asking what one more season might show you.

Even the ocean comes back to the same shore.`},"9:none":{title:"You tend to become part of someone’s days without a sound.",gist:"Your feelings grow through small, quiet habits and can stay so private that nobody sees them.",body:`Private and in no hurry, you tend to let feelings grow the way rain falls: softly, slowly, a little everywhere. Love, for you, often begins as a quiet friendship, one small habit at a time.

It might start with lending someone a charger, then sharing a playlist, then walking the same way home. Or knowing how a friend takes their tea before they ask.

The risk is that it stays invisible. You may be so gentle and private that the other person cannot tell what you feel, and sometimes you are not sure yourself. Putting it into one sentence, even only for yourself, is a good start.

Quiet love is still love, and it is allowed to be seen.`},"9:some":{title:"You tend to say the important things without words.",gist:"You read people without words, and when your quiet signals are missed, you may pull back.",body:`Patient, deep love tends to come naturally to you, and you understand people without words: their silences, their tired faces, the thing they are not saying. Your love can be like rain at night, the kind someone only notices in the morning.

It might be sending a song instead of a message, hoping the second verse says enough. Or slipping a small note into someone’s bag on a long day, with only a drawing on it.

You may hope to be read the same way. When quiet signals are missed, you can feel unseen and pull back. Try saying it once in plain words. It gives the other person a fair chance.

Not everyone hears the second verse.`},"9:many":{title:"People tend to feel you understand them better than anyone.",gist:"Many feel deeply understood by you, but when things get serious, you may slip quietly away.",body:`Gentle and intuitive, you tend to draw people in. You make others feel understood, so attention can reach you from many directions, softly, like rain.

It might be that several friends each know a different part of you. One knows your music, one knows your fears, and one has seen you laugh until you cried. Nobody quite has the whole picture.

Closeness can be the tricky part. When things get serious or difficult, you may step back quietly instead of saying what you want, and people can be left unsure. Try telling one person a little more than usual.

Rain falls everywhere. You can choose where it stays.`}},l=[{title:"In a shared home, you tend to feel safe with a plan.",gist:"Planning together helps you feel at home, but surprises can feel like problems.",body:`Your chart points to a home where things are thought through. If you share a space with a partner, you may feel safest when you both know what comes next, and late talks about plans can feel like closeness. A quick, curious partner who enjoys a good plan may suit you.

It might be a shared calendar with a colour for each of you. Or a cupboard with a spare of everything: tea, batteries, the good noodles. Maybe a winter evening spent planning something big, or a fairer way to split the chores.

Surprises can be the harder part, because they may feel like problems. Try leaving one evening a week unplanned, and see what fills it.

Some good things are not on the calendar.`},{title:"Shared routines tend to feel like love to you.",gist:"Steady routines make a home feel loving to you, but change may meet a quick no.",body:`You tend to build a home slowly and keep it with care, like a field looked after season after season. In a space shared with someone, small rituals can mean more to you than big events. A patient, reliable partner who keeps promises could be a good match.

It might be the same breakfast in the same two mugs. Or a Sunday walk to the same bakery, where they know the order by heart. Maybe a shelf built together over three weekends.

Stubbornness can be the cost. When someone wants to move the furniture or try a new café, a no may come before you have really listened. Try “Tell me more” first.

Routines are a gift. Leave room for one new thing.`},{title:"Home tends to feel best to you as a base camp.",gist:"You tend to need freedom inside closeness and act fast, so a partner may feel left behind.",body:`In a shared home, you tend to need freedom inside closeness. You can be fiercely protective of the people close to you, and you also need room to roam. A brave, independent partner with a life of their own tends to fit well.

It might be a bag by the door that never gets fully unpacked. Or a plan that appears at ten at night: “Let’s go and watch the sunrise.” By five in the morning, maybe two of you are on a cold hill with something hot to drink.

Speed is the thing to watch. You may decide fast and act faster, and the person beside you can feel left behind. Try asking, “Are you in?” before you go.

Adventure is better shared.`},{title:"Peace and small beauty tend to matter to you at home.",gist:"You keep home peaceful and lovely, but small problems may stay unspoken until they grow.",body:`Calm rooms and small, beautiful things tend to matter to you, and you create both easily. In a home shared with a partner, you may like voices soft and small touches noticed. Someone gentle and thoughtful may feel right.

It might be a lamp with warm light, cushions in two calm colours, or a plant moved three times until the morning sun finds it. Maybe you are the first to see when a cushion is out of place.

Arguments can be the hard part. “It’s fine” can stretch on for months, until something small has grown big. A short, calm talk early is often easier than a long one later.

A peaceful home can hold an honest word.`},{title:"Home, for you, tends to be where big plans are made.",gist:"A partner with big dreams may suit you, though small choices can turn into contests.",body:`Confident and ambitious, you tend to see a home as more than a place to sleep. It can be where a big life gets planned. A partner with goals of their own, who stands beside you rather than behind you, may fit well.

It might be a list of dreams on the fridge in two handwritings: learn to dive, start something of your own, see a faraway country. Or long talks at the kitchen table about what each of you wants to build.

In a shared home, two big presences can fill a room, and even the colour of the curtains can turn into a meeting. Try letting the other person decide one small thing fully.

Big dreams fit better with room for two.`},{title:"You tend to open your home, and your inner world, to few.",gist:"Sharing your private space is deep trust for you, but your worries may stay hidden.",body:`Letting someone into your home can be a deep form of trust for you. You tend to be wise and private, and not everyone gets past the door, which is fine. A partner who respects silence and keeps private things private could suit you.

It might be low lights and a quiet room where every object has a story. Maybe two people can sit together for an hour without talking and still feel close.

Sometimes privacy turns into distance. Worries kept well hidden can leave the person closest to you to guess. One small step: say one worry out loud each week, even a small one.

Your inner room is safe. A wider door can be safe too.`},{title:"You tend to like a home with open windows and movement.",gist:"A lively, honest home suits you, but slow, quiet days can make you restless.",body:`Air and movement tend to matter to you at home, and a very quiet routine can feel tight. Honest talk matters too, even when it gets loud. Someone who loves life, says what they think and is ready to go out is often a good match.

It might be music from another room, friends who drop by without texting first, or a late trip out for ice cream on a warm night. Maybe two people talk about everything, sometimes from different rooms.

Sitting still is the harder part. A slow Sunday can make you restless while someone close to you wants a quiet day in. Now and then, staying in on purpose can be its own small adventure.

Movement keeps love awake. So does stillness, shared.`},{title:"You tend to make a home soft, creative and kind.",gist:"Your care makes a home feel tender, though worry about how a partner feels can keep you awake.",body:`A home you shape tends to feel tender and creative, and you care deeply about how the people in it feel. A warm, patient partner who enjoys the small beauty you make may feel like home to you.

It might be a blanket on every chair, or walls covered in postcards and drawings, a few of them crooked. Maybe something good cooking, and small lights by the window.

Worry can follow you even here. You may lie awake wondering whether someone is truly happy, or whether you said the wrong thing. One small habit: end the day by naming one thing that went well between you.

Be as gentle with yourself as you are with your home.`},{title:"At home, you tend to need play as much as peace.",gist:"Games and new ideas keep home lively for you, and too much sameness can feel heavy.",body:`You tend to be clever, playful and inventive, and a shared home suits you best when it keeps your mind busy. A dull week can feel heavier than a hard one. A partner who laughs easily and brings ideas of their own may be a natural fit.

It might be a weekly game night with a scoreboard by the door. Or a huge puzzle on the table that nobody is allowed to touch, or a joke project that somehow becomes a real plan.

When life gets very calm, you may feel restless, and small things can start to bother you more than usual. Try giving that energy somewhere to go together: a new recipe, a game to learn, a small project.

Calm is where the next idea rests.`},{title:"Order and clear words tend to help you feel at home.",gist:"You like order and say things directly, but redoing a partner’s chores can sound like criticism.",body:`You tend to feel at peace where things have a place and agreements are clear. When something bothers you, you would rather say it the same day, plainly, than let it sit. Someone who respects your systems, and can hear direct words without feeling attacked, tends to fit well.

It might be labelled jars in a neat row, towels folded the same way every time, or a fair list for the chores. Maybe you are the one who always knows where the scissors are.

A sharp eye can turn into criticism. Folding towels again after someone else has folded them sends a message, even one you did not mean. Let one thing be done their way, and see how it feels.

A home is not a test.`},{title:"You tend to guard the people inside your door.",gist:"Your loyalty at home runs deep, and old hurts, yours or a partner’s, can stay with you.",body:`For you, a shared home can feel like a promise of loyalty. You tend to be honest and protective, and you stand up for the people inside your door with your whole heart. An honest, loyal partner whose word you never have to check may feel like the right match.

It might be remembering a rude joke about someone you love, years after they have forgotten it. Or being the first to say, “You were right, and they were wrong.”

Old hurts can stay with you, and a small broken promise may feel like a big betrayal. One habit that helps: when an old hurt comes back, ask whether it still needs guarding.

Guard the door, and let old hurts walk out of it.`},{title:"Home tends to mean a full table for you.",gist:"A generous table is how you share love, but quiet time for two can slip away.",body:`Love, for you, can taste like a shared meal. You tend to be warm, generous and sincere, and you like people to leave your home full in every way. A partner who enjoys good food and good people may suit you.

It might be a big pot in the middle of the table on a cold evening, and a friend who has brought another friend. Or the best piece going to someone you love without a word.

You may give and give. In a shared home, the rooms can fill with other people while quiet evenings for two keep moving to next week. Keeping one evening a week just for two can help.

A small table is cosy too.`}],u={"none:strong":{title:"You tend to work for the craft and forget the price.",gist:"Skill and pride move you more than money, so you may give your time away too easily.",body:`Your chart points to someone moved by skill and pride more than by money. You have plenty of energy, and you would rather use it well than count it. When money comes, it tends to come through what you can do, not through chasing it.

It might look like a favour that grows: a poster for a friend’s event, a cake for a birthday, a bike someone asked you to look at. You may say yes, do it properly, and only later notice that nobody mentioned payment.

The flip side is that your time can go too easily. If you rarely say what your work is worth, try saying it once, calmly and plainly.

Your skill has worth, even when you love using it.`},"none:balanced":{title:"You tend to choose good people over a bigger number.",gist:"Chances tend to reach you through people, but you may leave money unchecked for too long.",body:`Money is rarely the thing that decides for you. You tend to take it as it comes and weigh other things first: how a place feels, and who is there. Chances often reach you through people, like a recommendation, a favour returned or someone who remembers good work.

Maybe it is a choice between two teams, two classes or two shared flats. One has the better numbers. The other has people you like, and you tend to lean that way.

The flip side is drifting. You may not look at your money until something makes you, and then it can feel bigger than it is.

Choosing people is a kind of wisdom. Checking your money can be as calm as checking the weather.`},"none:weak":{title:"Asking to be paid back can feel harder than it should.",gist:"People matter more to you than money, so asking, charging or saying no can feel hard.",body:`People tend to move you more than money does. You care about how things feel between you and others, and good things often reach you through people who like and trust you, such as help that arrives just in time.

It might be a small sum a friend still owes you, and a message you start, then delete. Or a price you lower before anyone asks. Or a “yes” to helping out when you meant “not this week”.

The flip side is that asking, charging or saying no can feel hard, and you may doubt you deserve what is yours. Try asking for one small thing, kindly and plainly.

Kind people can ask for what they are owed. It does not make you less kind.`},"some:strong":{title:"Money tends to feel like a tool in your hand.",gist:"You handle money with a clear plan and energy, though other people’s ways can frustrate you.",body:`You tend to have a clear, practical way with money, and the energy to follow it. You know what you want and work towards it, and a choice made with open eyes rarely troubles you later.

It might be one thing you wait for, like a camera, a course or a trip. You might watch it for weeks, then decide in a minute and feel proud for days. Or a simple rule of your own, kept without fuss.

The flip side can be a little stubbornness. A friend who spends differently may puzzle you, and advice you did not ask for may be hard to hear. Ask one question before you judge.

Your way works for you. Leave some room for joy that was not in the plan.`},"some:balanced":{title:"You often know what things cost without keeping a list.",gist:"You handle money with calm common sense, but routine can keep a bigger wish waiting.",body:`Your chart points to a steady, practical feel for money. You tend to spend on what matters, skip what does not, and rarely panic. Money is not your enemy, and it is not your boss either.

It can look like small knowledge you never set out to learn. Maybe you know which market is cheaper for rice, or that the bakery lowers its prices near closing time.

The flip side is that the same patterns can run on their own. A small payment you no longer use might keep going for months, or a bigger wish might stay “someday” because everyday life works fine. A look now and then at what still fits can help.

Steady is a quiet strength. Let yourself want something bigger, too.`},"some:weak":{title:"Money and feeling safe tend to sit close together for you.",gist:"You are careful and practical with money, though worry can stay even when things are fine.",body:`Your chart suggests a careful, practical way with money. You tend to do best with simple habits and people you trust. You may also pick up other people’s feelings about money, such as a friend’s worry or a group plan that feels like too much.

It might be checking your balance before a small treat, just in case. Or ordering the small coffee when you wanted the large one.

The flip side is that worry can stay on good days too, and take small joys with it. If you notice you hold back long after you need to, name one small thing that is safe to enjoy this week.

You are more careful than you think. Small joys are allowed.`},"many:strong":{title:"You tend to see chances to earn where others see nothing.",gist:"You spot chances everywhere and enjoy being generous, though a good week can make you spend boldly.",body:`Money tends to move around you, and you have the energy to move with it. You often see chances where others see nothing: something to sell, a deal to make, a gap nobody has filled. Big numbers, coming or going, do not scare you much.

It might be an idea for something to sell before lunch, and three more by dinner. Or a good week that ends with you treating the whole table.

The flip side is that a good week can make you bold. Big wins can invite big spending, and you may take on more than anyone could carry. Letting one night pass before a big yes can help.

Enjoy the flow. Just notice where it goes.`},"many:balanced":{title:"New interests can pull you in fast, and so can the shop.",gist:"Money comes and goes easily for you, though small costs for new interests can quietly add up.",body:`Money tends to flow in and out for you through many small doors: maybe a shift, a gift, something sold or a small prize. You go with it easily. You enjoy chances when they come, adjust when they go, and money rarely keeps you awake.

New interests pull you in quickly too. It might be a sport one month and a craft the next. A quick look in a shop can end with paints, a racket or a pasta machine, and a head full of plans.

The flip side is that small costs add up quietly, and a cupboard can fill with past passions. Before you pay, one question can help: “Will I still love this next month?”

Try things freely, and keep the ones that stay.`},"many:weak":{title:"Other people’s money and plans tend to land in your hands.",gist:"People trust you with money and plans, but you may carry their worries and say yes when unsure.",body:`A lot tends to pass through your hands: chances, requests and sometimes other people’s money. People trust you with it, and you are thoughtful and fair. You tend to do best with good people beside you and simple habits that carry some of the weight.

It might be the money for a group gift, plus a few people who will “pay tomorrow”. Or a shared bill that ends up on your phone, or a trip someone asks you to organise.

The flip side is that it can feel heavy. Other people’s money worries can become yours, and you may say yes when you are unsure. It is fine to say “Let me think” first.

You do not have to carry it alone. Ask someone you trust to hold one end.`}},d=[{title:"Somewhere new, you tend to look quick and ready.",gist:"People may first see someone quick and sure, and forget you might need help too.",body:`You tend to come across as quick and capable, especially in a new place. You often work out how a place runs, who knows what and where things are, and people may notice that before anything else.

It might be your second day in a new class or job, and you already know the quickest way to the station. Or maybe you are the one who remembers everyone’s names, or who plans where the group meets next.

The other side is that you can look so sure that people forget to offer help. On the days you feel lost, it may not show. One simple question early on can also be an easy way to get to know someone.

Being helped can be the start of a friendship too.`},{title:"Your first impression tends to be quiet, then grows over time.",gist:"People may first find you serious, then trust you more as you do what you say.",body:`You tend to make an impression slowly. At first you may say less than others and show who you are through what you do, not through big words. Patience and steady effort are often what people come to rely on.

It can look like a shared task where you ask, “Who does what, and by when?” and then quietly finish your part early. Or a friend who notices, weeks later, that you remembered exactly what you promised.

A quiet start has a cost, though: people who do not know you yet may read it as cold or stubborn. A small smile or one friendly question at the beginning can help them see you sooner.

Your steadiness speaks over time. Let a little warmth speak first.`},{title:"People tend to notice your courage before anything else.",gist:"Your courage tends to show first, though quick words can come before the full story.",body:`Courage is often the first thing people see in you. You tend to move first, say what others are only thinking and stand beside anyone who is treated unfairly. New people can feel safer with you nearby.

Maybe it is the quiet moment after “Any questions?”, when yours is the first hand up. Or a small, firm “That’s not okay” when someone in the group is spoken to rudely.

The flip side is that strength like yours can feel like a lot to people who have just met you. And quick words can come before you know the whole story. Try one slow breath, or one question, before you step in.

Keep the courage, and give it one breath before it speaks.`},{title:"You tend to look calm, kind and easy to approach.",gist:"People often find you gentle and easy to approach, and may not see when you disagree.",body:`There is a softness people may notice in you right away. You tend to smooth things over, find the polite word and notice who feels left out. There can be a quiet good taste about you too, in small choices most people miss.

Perhaps a stranger picks you to ask the way at a busy station. Or, on a first day somewhere new, someone sits down next to you without quite knowing why.

What to notice: a gentle face can hide a different opinion. People may think you agree when you do not, or miss it when something hurts. A soft “I see it a little differently” now and then can help.

Kindness and a clear no can live side by side.`},{title:"You tend to walk in looking like you are in charge.",gist:"Your confident presence can make people expect you to lead, and never to feel nervous.",body:`You tend to carry yourself with confidence, and people may sense it before you say a word. You often think big, speak clearly and fill a room without trying.

Maybe, at a first meeting, heads turn to you as if you were leading, even when you know as little as anyone. Or someone at a party asks you where the coats go, as if it were your party.

The harder part is that people may expect a lot and assume you are never nervous. That can feel lonely on unsure days. One honest “I’m not sure yet”, said to someone you trust, can make it lighter.

You can be big and unsure on the same day.`},{title:"You tend to watch first, and speak when it counts.",gist:"People may see you as calm and a little mysterious, and read your quiet as distance.",body:`Your chart suggests a calm, private first impression. You tend to watch before you speak, notice what people leave unsaid and share yourself slowly. Others may see someone composed, elegant and a little hard to read.

For example, in a new group chat you might read every message and reply once, with one calm sentence that changes the direction of the talk. Or someone may tell you, half laughing, “I never know what you’re thinking.”

On the other hand, quiet can look like judgement to people who have just met you, and some may decide you are cold. Sharing one small, easy thing about yourself early can open the door.

Keep your depth. Let one or two people see in.`},{title:"Talking to strangers tends to come easily to you.",gist:"People often find you open and honest, though your speed can make them wonder if you will stay.",body:`Openness is often what people meet first in you. You tend to be quick, honest and full of energy, and you do not like to pretend. People may find you easy to talk to from the first minute.

It might be a long line or a late bus, and ten minutes later you are sharing stories with a stranger. Or, in a slow meeting, you are the one who says what others only think: “Could this be shorter?”

The other side is that so much energy can feel like a lot, fast. And because you move on quickly, some people may wonder whether you will stay. It can help to stay a little longer than feels natural.

Some friendships open slowly, and they are worth the wait.`},{title:"You tend to make new people feel welcome, then worry afterwards.",gist:"People often find you gentle and welcoming, though afterwards you may replay every word you said.",body:`Your gentleness may reach people before your words do. You tend to ask kind questions, remember small details and make quieter people feel included.

It might show in a question you ask the shy person at the edge of a group, or a detail you remember from last week. Maybe there is something artistic about you too: the colours you choose, or the way you describe an ordinary sky.

Afterwards, though, you may go over every sentence. “Did I talk too much? Was that joke strange?” If you notice that replay starting, try ending the day with one thing that went well.

You were probably kinder than you think. Let that be the last thought of the day.`},{title:"Your quick humour tends to help new people relax.",gist:"Your humour can help people relax, though some may miss your serious ideas at first.",body:`A quick, playful mind is often what people notice early on. You tend to find the funny side of things, play with ideas and help a stiff moment loosen up.

It could be the first online call of a new group, every camera off, until one small joke about the mess behind you gets people laughing. Or a fresh idea when a conversation is stuck, and suddenly everyone is talking.

Where it can cost you: people may not take you seriously at first, and slow, repeated tasks can leave you restless. One habit to try: after the joke, share one serious thought, so they see both sides.

Be funny, and let your ideas be heard too.`},{title:"You tend to look organised from the moment you arrive.",gist:"People may see you as capable and organised, though your directness can sound like criticism.",body:`People may see someone capable as soon as they meet you. You tend to like things done properly, notice details others miss and say what you think simply. To new people you can seem organised, sure and a little strict.

Maybe it is a new club or team, and you arrive a few minutes early with a pen and some ideas written down. Or you are the one who quietly spots the wrong date on a poster, and you are right.

The flip side is that directness can sound like criticism to people who do not know you yet, and some may think you are hard to please. Try naming one thing that works before what could be better.

Keep your standards, and share them with a smile.`},{title:"You tend to be kind at once, and slow to trust.",gist:"You tend to be friendly to everyone but slow to trust, which some may read as distance.",body:`Polite, honest and helpful: that tends to be your first impression, with trust coming later. You often watch how people treat others before you let them close. Once someone is in, your loyalty can last for years.

Imagine a new class or workplace: you hold the door, explain how things work and help carry something heavy. Then someone suggests dinner, and you smile and say, “Next time.”

A slow start can look like distance, though. Some people may think you do not like them and stop trying. A small step can help: say yes to one short coffee and see how it feels.

Your trust is worth earning. Give a few more people the chance.`},{title:"You tend to say the kind thing out loud.",gist:"People often feel your warmth quickly, though not everyone gives the same warmth back.",body:`Warmth tends to come easily to you, and people can often feel it from the start. You often see the good in others and say so. Your smile usually means what it says.

You might tell someone at the next table that their jacket looks great, and mean it. Maybe you learn names fast, ask about someone’s weekend and really listen to the answer, or share whatever snacks you have.

Not everyone answers an open heart with the same warmth, and when they do not, it can stay with you. It can help to let trust take a little longer than kindness.

Keep saying the kind thing. It matters more than it may seem.`}],y=[{title:"In private, you tend to be the quiet planner.",gist:"At home you tend to plan a few steps ahead, and your mind can keep listing at night.",body:`In private, you tend to be the one who plans. Your mind often runs a few steps ahead, and being ready helps you and the people close to you feel at ease.

Perhaps you are the one who always seems to know where the spare key is, or how much rice is left. Maybe friends ask you for a birthday or a phone number instead of searching, because you probably have it.

The flip side can show up at night, when your mind keeps going through lists: tomorrow, next week, what might go wrong. Try writing the list on paper before bed, so it has somewhere to wait.

The list will still be there in the morning. You can rest now.`},{title:"You tend to show care through quiet, steady actions.",gist:"You tend to show love through actions, and sudden changes to plans can make you go quiet.",body:`At home you tend to be steady and practical. You often show care with your hands more than your words, and a familiar rhythm is how you rest.

This can look like the same cup and the same quiet first half hour of the day. Or fixing the drawer that sticks, or doing a job nobody asked you to do, and never mentioning it.

There can be a stubborn side too. When a plan changes suddenly, you may go quiet for a while. Saying “Give me a minute with this” lets people know the silence is not about them.

Say the kind things out loud sometimes. People like to hear them.`},{title:"When you feel stuck, you tend to change something fast.",gist:"You tend to need room to move and protect your people fiercely, though moods can shift quickly.",body:`Your private self tends to be bold and protective, with moods that move fast. You may need space to move and the freedom to be yourself, and you can be fierce about the people you care for.

When you feel stuck, you might move the furniture around late in the evening, try a new haircut or take a different way home. Or, if someone hurts a person you love, you may be ready to stand up for them that same night.

The flip side is that your moods can change faster than others can follow. A walk without a word might leave someone wondering what happened. Before you go, try a simple “I just need some air.”

Space is easier to give when people know why you need it.`},{title:"At home, you would often rather smooth things over than argue.",gist:"You tend to choose peace over being right, so small problems can stay unspoken too long.",body:`Calm tends to matter more to you than being right. You feel the mood of a room and smooth it, often before anyone else notices there was a problem.

If you share a space, it might look like washing someone else’s cups instead of saying something, and telling yourself it is fine. With friends, maybe a small hurt passes without a word, and the evening goes on as if nothing happened.

What to watch for: small things can grow when nobody names them. It can help to say the small thing early, while it is still small, in your own kind words.

A gentle home has room for honest words too.`},{title:"Your dreams tend to grow to full size at home.",gist:"At home your dreams can grow huge, though resting can be harder for you than planning.",body:`Home is often where your biggest plans take shape. Away from other people’s eyes, your dreams have room to be as large as they really are, and the people close to you may hear them first.

You might practise a speech in the shower, or answer an imaginary interview perfectly. Maybe a new plan arrives over breakfast: a trip, a project, a whole new direction.

The harder part can be rest. Home may start to feel like one more stage, where you are always preparing for something. Try one evening with nothing to plan and nothing to prove.

Behind big dreams, a few words of belief from one close person can mean a lot.`},{title:"You tend to hear what people mean, not only what they say.",gist:"You tend to sense feelings early and need private time, though few read you as easily.",body:`With the people closest to you, you tend to be deep and quietly wise. You often sense how someone feels before they say it. You may also need a closed door of your own: time alone and thoughts you keep to yourself.

Someone close might say, “I’m fine,” while you hear the tired voice and see how they put their bag down. Maybe you do not push. You make tea, sit nearby and wait until they are ready.

The other side is that you can understand others while few people understand you as easily. Even close friends may wonder how you really feel. Try sharing one feeling of your own, not only what you notice.

They want to know you too.`},{title:"Even at home, freedom tends to matter a lot to you.",gist:"Freedom tends to matter even in private, so close people may mostly see your rushed side.",body:`You tend to need freedom to feel like yourself, even with the people you love most. You are often honest with the people close to you, and you can bring fresh energy home each time you come back.

It might be a bag by the door that never gets fully unpacked, or dinner eaten standing up between one plan and the next. Maybe you come home full of stories and tell them all at once.

The flip side is that the people closest to you may mostly see the tired, rushed you, and honesty without softness can sting. One unhurried evening together, and a softer word when the truth is hard, can help.

A simple “I’ll be back at nine” can mean more than it seems.`},{title:"You tend to keep small kindnesses close for years.",gist:"You tend to treasure small kindnesses for years, though old worries can be kept just as carefully.",body:`Tenderness tends to show most when you are in private. You often remember small kindnesses for years, and you like to give them back in your own way.

Maybe there is a box of old cards and tickets you open on a quiet evening. Or a handmade gift, a song you chose for someone, soup brought over on a sad day.

Worries can be kept the same way, though. Old hurts and small fears may be stored as carefully as the cards. One small habit: write a worry down, then close the notebook on it for the night.

Keep the cards. The worries can go.`},{title:"Home tends to be where your playful side runs free.",gist:"You tend to keep home light and playful, though a joke can come before a serious talk.",body:`In private you are often at your most playful. You tend to be quick and clever at home, and you can turn a dull evening into a small adventure.

Perhaps the people close to you have nicknames that change every few months, or the fridge gets a funny voice when it hums. Maybe you fix something broken in a way nobody expected, with a hair clip and some tape.

Notice, though, that a joke may come first when someone needs a serious talk. It can help to stay with the serious moment a little longer, and save the funny line for after.

Laughter is a gift. So is listening all the way through.`},{title:"You tend to notice what is out of place at once.",gist:"Your eye for detail helps, though corrections can sound like complaints to the people you love.",body:`You tend to like things in their place. You often have a sharp eye and care about doing things properly. With people close to you, you are usually honest and direct, and fixing small things can be one way you show care.

It might be noticing, from the doorway, that a picture hangs a little to the left. Or a towel folded the other way, or a cap left off the toothpaste, fixed before your bag is even down.

A correction can sound like a complaint, though. People may hear “wrong” when you mean “I care.” Saying sorry first may not come easily either. Try saying thank you before you fix anything.

Your care is real. Let it be heard first.`},{title:"You tend to stay loyal, and sometimes stay angry for others.",gist:"You tend to be deeply loyal, and may stay upset for your people long after they forgive.",body:`Loyalty tends to run deep in you, especially with the people closest to you. You often protect them, tell them the truth even when it is hard, and keep their secrets.

For example, you may stay awake until someone sends a message that they got home safely. Or a friend mentions a person who hurt them long ago and laughs about it, while you notice you are still not ready to.

Sometimes you may carry anger your friends have already put down, and your honesty can hurt more than you mean. When they have let it go, try letting it go a little too.

Your loyalty is a gift, and it does not need to carry old anger.`},{title:"You tend to show love by making sure everyone is fed.",gist:"You tend to look after people with food and comfort, and may forget to ask for anything back.",body:`Generosity tends to show most in your private life. You are often sincere and warm, and you show love in simple ways: making sure people have eaten, are comfortable and feel welcome.

It might be remembering who likes their tea sweet and who cannot stand onions. Maybe you send a message just to ask whether someone has eaten yet.

You can give and give, though, and forget to ask for anything back. A hard conversation may also wait too long while the mood is good. Try letting someone look after you for once, even if they cook badly.

You deserve the same care you give.`}],g={甲子:{title:"When a question gets hard, people often turn to you.",gist:"You learn quietly and explain clearly, though people may forget to ask how you are.",body:`{name}, you tend to learn quietly and all the time. You like to understand how a thing works before you say much about it, so when you do speak, people often trust what you say and let you lead.

It might be a friend who brings you a confusing set of rules to untangle, or a question you keep reading about long after others have moved on. Maybe a group goes quiet over a hard choice, and then everyone looks your way.

The other side is that people who lean on your answers may forget to ask how you are. It can help to say it first: one honest line about your own week is enough.

Keep learning. Let someone teach you something, too.`},甲寅:{title:"You often prefer to carry the heavy part yourself.",gist:"You rely on your own strength, and people may stop offering help you could use.",body:`{name}, standing on your own comes naturally to you. You trust your own strength and your own way of doing things, and you tend to dislike being told what to do. People often see you as the steady one.

It can look like carrying every bag home from the shop when someone offers to take one, or building a new shelf alone with the instructions spread on the floor. Maybe it is a problem you quietly solve before anyone knows there is one.

The flip side is that people may stop offering. When help is turned down often enough, others can decide you never need it. Try saying yes to one small offer this week, and see how it feels.

Even a tall tree grows in a forest.`},甲辰:{title:"Where others see a seed, you often see the whole tree.",gist:"You turn small chances into real projects, but too many yeses can leave little time to rest.",body:`{name}, your chart suggests practical ambition: the energy to start, and the sense to turn a start into something that lasts. When you spot a chance, you tend to grow it.

It might be a small favour for a friend that turns into something many people ask you for, or a one-time get-together that becomes a yearly one with you at the centre.

What can get hard is the pace. Each new idea can feel worth a yes, and free time may quietly turn into work. When you are tired, your words can come out sharper than you mean. One small habit: keep one thing in your week that has no goal at all.

Grow it as big as you like.`},甲午:{title:"When something moves you, it tends to show right away.",gist:"You say what you feel warmly and quickly, though the speed can sting and leave you drained.",body:`{name}, your feelings tend to travel fast from the inside to the outside. When something moves you, you say it, warmly and honestly, and that openness can lift a whole room.

Maybe it is a few words at a goodbye that nobody else was ready to say. Or a hug and a loud cheer the moment a friend shares good news.

The other side is the speed. A true thing said too quickly can hurt more than you meant, and giving so much at once can leave you empty the next day. It can help to count to five before the honest part, and to plan a quiet day after a big one.

Your warmth is a gift. Pace it, so it lasts.`},甲申:{title:"When things go wrong, you tend to get calmer and clearer.",gist:"You stay steady and careful under pressure, though you may hold the tension long after.",body:`{name}, hard moments often make you steadier. Pressure seems to make you careful and clear rather than loud, and you tend to hold yourself to high standards. People can feel that calm and lean on it.

Picture a cancelled bus and a confused crowd: you may already be checking the next route. Or a plan may fall apart the day before, and by evening you have quietly made a new one.

The cost can come later. You may carry the tension long after everyone else has let it go, and judge yourself more strictly than anyone else would. Try closing a difficult day with something small, like a song or a few lines in a notebook.

A pine bends in the wind and still stands.`},甲戌:{title:"Your loyalty often shows in your hands more than your words.",gist:"You show care through practical help, though you may give more than you take and remember hurts.",body:`{name}, loyalty is strong in you, and it tends to show through what you do. Once someone matters to you, you turn up, and you often help in practical ways rather than with big speeches.

It can look like a moving day where you arrive with tape and a plan for the sofa, and leave long after dark. Or a friend’s big day, where you set up the chairs before anyone asks.

Balance can be the hard part. You may give much more than you take, and an old hurt can stay with you for years. Let someone return a favour now and then, and try saying a hurt out loud once, early, instead of keeping it.

Keep showing up for people. Let them show up for you.`},乙丑:{title:"Small steps, taken every day, can carry you far.",gist:"Patient daily effort takes you far, but the work behind it often goes unseen.",body:`{name}, you often move forward in small, steady steps. You do not need a big start. You seem to know that a little, done every day, adds up, and you tend to be careful with what you have.

Maybe it is ten minutes of a new language while waiting in a queue, long after others who started with you have stopped. Or a sketchbook that fills one page a day, or a skill that grows so slowly that only you notice.

What others may not see is the effort, and they may assume it came easily. You may also keep going with something after it has stopped helping you. Now and then, ask whether this path still deserves your steps.

Slow is still moving.`},乙卯:{title:"Gentle on the outside, you quietly go your own way.",gist:"You listen kindly, then follow your own taste, often before others know you disagreed.",body:`{name}, you are soft on the outside and quite sure on the inside. Like spring grass, you tend to bend when people push, then stand again in your own way.

Maybe it is a haircut, a paint colour or a route for a trip. Friends may offer advice, and you nod and thank them. Later you may choose something else, and it often suits you well.

The other side is the quiet before. People may not know you disagreed until it is already done. With friends who are a lot like you, a small, silent contest may also grow. Try saying “I see it differently” early, in a light voice.

Your way is often a good way. Let people know it is coming.`},乙巳:{title:"You often see how to make something look just right.",gist:"You have an eye for beauty and style, though the same eye can be hard on flaws.",body:`{name}, you have a quick eye for what is beautiful, and often a feel for how to show it. Colour, light and the right words tend to come to you without much effort.

On a day out, friends may hand you their phone because your photos come out well. Or you might be the one who moves two things on a table, and suddenly the whole room looks finished.

The same eye also sees what is wrong, and you may say it more sharply than you plan. Messy feelings can be harder for you to show than pretty things. Try sharing one unpolished thought with someone you trust.

Some moments are beautiful because they are messy.`},乙未:{title:"You often make something good out of very little.",gist:"You are kind and resourceful with little, though worry can make good days hard to enjoy.",body:`{name}, you tend to find a way with what is already there. You are kind, practical and patient, and you keep going quietly when things are hard.

It might be a dinner made from three leftover things that somehow feeds four people, or a birthday card drawn by hand instead of bought. Maybe it is an old jacket that gets a new life from a few stitches.

The flip side is worry. You may prepare for bad days so often that the good ones are hard to enjoy, and you may accept “enough” for yourself when you could ask for more. One small habit: when a good day comes, name it out loud.

You are allowed to have plenty, not only enough.`},乙酉:{title:"The one thing out of place is often the first you see.",gist:"You look gentle but hold strict standards, and a small mistake can stay with you for days.",body:`{name}, you may look gentle, but you tend to hold yourself to clear, strict standards. Clean lines, neat edges and things done properly often matter to you, and pressure usually makes you more careful.

Perhaps you straighten a picture frame in a friend’s hallway without thinking, or spot the one typo on a printed menu, or fold every shirt so the edges meet.

What can get hard is how tight the rules become. A small mistake, yours or someone else’s, may sting for days, and you may hide your nerves so well that nobody offers help. Try letting one small thing stay imperfect on purpose, and notice that nothing breaks.

Some things can just be soft.`},乙亥:{title:"You tend to trust the current and see where it goes.",gist:"You are open, warm and trusting, though you may drift and let your own wishes go quiet.",body:`{name}, you seem to move through the world with an open hand. You are gentle and easy to talk to, you understand things by feeling them, and you often trust where the day takes you.

It might be a short walk that turns into a long chat with someone on a bench, or a stranger asking you for directions and leaving with the name of a good café too. Maybe a free afternoon fills itself, and you are happy to let it.

Drifting can be the cost. Plans can melt, decisions can wait, and you may lean on others so much that your own wishes go quiet. It can help to write down one thing you want each week.

Keep floating. Just know where the shore is.`},丙子:{title:"Your warmth tends to come with good manners and kept promises.",gist:"You are warm in a careful, reliable way, and your mind may find it hard to rest.",body:`{name}, your chart points to warmth with a sense of duty: a light that shines in a careful, proper way. Kept promises, good manners and being on time often matter to you, and people tend to trust you with things that must be done right.

Perhaps it is a handwritten note after a nice evening, or arriving ten minutes early. Or you may be the one who remembers to send the photos to everyone afterwards.

Under a calm face, your mind may be busy. You may replay a conversation to check you said the right thing, and resting before everything is done can feel hard. Try ending the day with one line: “That was enough.”

Your light is enough, even on a day you forget something.`},丙寅:{title:"New beginnings tend to light you up, and others follow.",gist:"Your excitement for fresh starts draws people in, though your interest may fade before the end.",body:`{name}, beginnings tend to wake you up. You are hopeful and brave, with ideas that often arrive faster than you can write them down, and your excitement spreads easily to the people around you.

Perhaps it is a sunrise walk you suggest, and somehow everyone says yes. Or the first week of a new class, when you are already asking for more. It can also look like a group chat that comes alive the moment you post a plan.

The flip side is that you may start before you check the map, and your interest can fade once the first glow does. Hearing “no” may also be hard. Pick one plan each season and carry it all the way to the end.

Keep starting things. Finish a few, too.`},丙辰:{title:"Any gathering you join can get bigger, warmer and better fed.",gist:"You love giving and making days bigger, though you may give until you have nothing left.",body:`{name}, giving tends to make you happy. Like a sun over an open field, you warm everyone at once, without checking who deserves it, and you have a gift for making a plain day feel like an occasion.

It can look like a picnic for four where you bring enough food for ten, and the people on the next blanket end up joining in. Or a small birthday that you turn into an evening everyone remembers.

The other side is that you can give until you run out. You may say yes to everyone, and then feel low if nobody notices how much went into it. Keep something for yourself first: an hour, a plate, a quiet evening.

Fill your own plate, then feed the park.`},丙午:{title:"You often live at full speed and full warmth.",gist:"You give and act at full speed, though waiting is hard and tiredness can sharpen your words.",body:`{name}, your energy tends to be bright and fast, and it lives in the present. You give generously, laugh loudly and stand up for your people without a long pause to think.

Maybe it is a voice message you answer before it has finished playing, or a weekend plan that goes from idea to guest list in an hour. You may often be the first to say “I’m in.”

Waiting can be the hard part. Not everyone moves at your speed, and when you are tired, a sharp word may come out before you can stop it. When you feel rushed, one slow breath before you answer can change a lot.

Not every hour has to be noon.`},丙申:{title:"Busy places often bring out your quick, friendly side.",gist:"You are quick, curious and easy to like, but moving fast can leave things half done.",body:`{name}, people often find you quick, curious and easy to like. You move lightly through busy places, you notice chances that others miss, and you can often tell a good idea from a weak one at a glance.

It could be a trip to a market for one thing that ends with you knowing three sellers by name. Or a chat in a waiting room that turns into a useful tip and a new contact.

Slowing down may not come easily. Many things get started and some stay half done, and giving your warmth to so many people can leave you empty by evening. Try finishing one small thing before you start the next.

Keep moving. Stop now and then to fill up again.`},丙戌:{title:"You are often the one who speaks up when something is unfair.",gist:"You protect people and stay loyal, though anger on someone’s behalf may stay long after.",body:`{name}, fairness matters a great deal to you. Your warmth is like the evening sun: softer than noon, but it reaches the whole room. You tend to protect people who cannot easily protect themselves, and once someone is yours, you stay loyal.

It could be a calm “That is enough” when someone is rude to a waiter, or a few clear words for a friend nobody lets finish a sentence. Maybe it is simply sitting next to the person who has been left out.

The cost can be the heat that stays. Unfairness to others may feel like your own, and the anger can last after they have moved on. Quiet time alone after a big day may help.

Keep standing up for people. Keep your evenings soft.`},丁丑:{title:"You can keep working quietly long after others have stopped.",gist:"You stay patient with hard tasks, and asking for help can come late.",body:`{name}, there is a patient, steady flame in you. You tend to keep going quietly when others give up, and you do not need anyone to clap. The moment something works again can be reward enough.

It could be an old lamp that everyone else wanted to throw away, or a knot in a necklace that takes an evening to undo.

You can also be stubborn. You may spend three days alone on a problem that one question could solve, and keep your feelings packed away until someone pushes too hard and a fierce side shows. Try asking for help on day one, not day three.

Fix what you love. Not everything is yours to fix.`},丁卯:{title:"You often take in the world slowly, and very deeply.",gist:"You notice what others miss and think deeply, but your best ideas may stay unspoken.",body:`{name}, the world seems to reach you slowly and deeply. You notice small, soft details that others walk past, ideas come to you from unusual directions, and your taste is quietly your own.

Maybe your friends finish a whole museum while you are still in front of one painting near the door. Or a single line in a song stays with you for a week, and you keep finding new meaning in it.

At times you can get lost inside your head. You may feel a lot and say little, and your most unusual ideas may stay with you alone. Share one of them with someone who enjoys odd thoughts.

Someone out there is waiting for exactly that idea.`},丁巳:{title:"You can look relaxed while quietly playing to win.",gist:"You have a strong, quiet drive to win, and losses may replay in your head at night.",body:`{name}, your chart points to more heat inside than people expect. You tend to watch quietly, read the next move and trust your own strength, and you often like to win in your own way.

It might be a relaxed board game where you joke and laugh, then win three moves before anyone notices. Or a quiz app that you keep playing until your name sits at the top of the list.

Almost anything can turn into a contest, and that can get tiring. After a loss, you may play the game again in your head at night, and strong feelings can flare up fast. Before you start, try asking: does this one need winning?

Win when it matters. Let the rest just be a game.`},丁未:{title:"Your warmth tends to make people want to stay a little longer.",gist:"You care for everyone and plan for every problem, yet being cared for can feel harder.",body:`{name}, your warmth is the kind that makes people want to stay. You tend to notice who is cold, who is quiet and who needs a chair, and you can make almost any place feel like home.

Perhaps it is an evening outside that turns chilly, when you are the one with a spare blanket and something hot to drink. Or a friend’s new room that feels warmer after one visit from you, with a lamp moved and a cushion found.

You may also worry more than you show, packing for every problem. Caring for others may come easily, while letting them care for you feels harder. Next time someone offers, try saying “Yes, thank you.”

You are allowed to sit closest to the fire, too.`},丁酉:{title:"You can often sense quality before you see the label.",gist:"You have a fine eye for quality, though “good enough” can feel like a small failure.",body:`{name}, you have a fine eye for quality. Like a candle next to a jewel, you bring out the shine in small things: a good word, a good colour, a detail done well.

Maybe in a shop your hand goes straight to the best-made thing before you have looked at the price. Or in a long letter, you notice the one word that was chosen with real care.

Your standards can be very high, for yourself too. You may see the scratch before the gift, and “good enough” can feel like a small failure. When someone questions your taste, it may sting more than it shows. Try naming one thing each day that is good as it is.

Use your eye gently, on yourself too.`},丁亥:{title:"People often find it easy to open up to you.",gist:"Your close attention makes people feel safe, though their worries can stay with you.",body:`{name}, your chart points to a close, steady kind of warmth: the attention of someone who notices how people really are. Near that kind of attention, people tend to relax and say more than they planned.

It might be a late talk in a kitchen, a long train ride or a message at midnight. You tend to listen without rushing to fix things, and you remember the small details that matter to people.

The flip side is that their worries can stay with you after they leave. If you notice you feel heavy after a day of listening, give your own feelings some room too.

Keep listening. A candle burns longest when it has air.`},戊子:{title:"People often trust you to keep things safe and in order.",gist:"You keep plans, promises and people safe, though surprises can feel more like trouble than fun.",body:`{name}, you tend to be steady and practical, like a mountain with a quiet lake at its feet. You keep things safe: plans, promises and the people close to you.

Maybe on a group trip you are the one keeping track of who paid for what. Or the spare key, the charger and the tissues are all in your bag the moment someone needs them.

The flip side is that a full plan can leave little room for surprises, and you may hold on a little tight. You may also put the people you love first and yourself last. One small habit: leave one part of a plan empty on purpose.

Keep the plan. Leave room for a good surprise.`},戊寅:{title:"People often feel safer when you are nearby.",gist:"You take responsibility for people’s safety, though you may hide your own fear and blame yourself first.",body:`{name}, your chart suggests a protective strength: solid ground with a brave guard on top. People tend to feel safer when you are near, and they often follow your lead without being asked.

It can show at the end of an evening out, when you make sure each friend has a way home before you leave. Or in a small emergency, like a lost child in a park, where you stay calm and act first.

The weight can add up. You may carry everyone’s safety on your shoulders, find it hard to show fear, and blame yourself first when something goes wrong. Being brave can include telling someone that you are scared.

Let someone walk you home sometimes.`},戊辰:{title:"You often think in years, and people lean on that.",gist:"People follow your big, steady plans, yet you may carry too much alone and rarely change course.",body:`{name}, you have a steady presence and big plans. You stand firm, you think in years rather than days, and people often lean on you without quite deciding to. When you walk into a room, it may settle around you.

Maybe you join a club for fun, and by the third meeting you have a plan for the whole year. Or a group project where everyone slowly starts looking at you for the next step.

The other side is that once you decide, you rarely move. You may carry too much alone, and people may feel small next to how sure you are. Try asking one question before you share your plan: “What would you do?”

Stay solid. Leave a path so others can climb with you.`},戊午:{title:"You can hold a lot inside, calmly, for a long time.",gist:"You stay patient and generous for a long time, though held-in feelings may come out all at once.",body:`{name}, you tend to be solid and quiet outside, with plenty of heat inside. You take in a lot and hold it for a long time. People often know you as patient, generous and honest.

It might be weeks of small things, like late replies or plans that change at the last minute, met with calm. Then one small thing, a door left open or a cup in the wrong place, and it all comes out at once.

People who only knew your calm can be surprised, and afterwards the right words may be hard to find. It can help to say small things while they are still small: one short, honest sentence a day.

A pot with the lid a little open does not boil over.`},戊申:{title:"In hard moments, you tend to find a fix and a joke.",gist:"Your ideas and humour make hard moments lighter, though your serious side can stay unseen.",body:`{name}, your chart points to a practical, playful mind. You tend to be inventive with whatever is nearby, and your ideas and humour can fix more than people expect. Hard moments often feel lighter with you in them.

Think of a power cut: you may be the one who finds the candles first and then invents a card game for the stairs. Or a broken bag strap gets fixed with a paper clip.

Slow days can be harder for you, and you may jump to a new idea early. A joke can also arrive where a serious word was needed, and people may miss how much you carry. Now and then, let the serious sentence come first.

Your fun is a gift. So is your depth.`},戊戌:{title:"When you choose something, you tend to choose it completely.",gist:"You stay loyal to places, people and beliefs, and broken trust can be hard to mend.",body:`{name}, your choices tend to be whole-hearted: a place, a friend, a belief. Like an old mountain, you are steady and loyal, with a quiet inner room where you think about big questions alone.

For some, it is the same café for years, where the staff start your usual order as you walk in. For others, it is one friendship that has lasted through many moves and still feels the same.

With you, it can be all or nothing. Once you leave, you may rarely return, and broken trust can be very hard to repair. Changing your mind may take a long time. Try leaving a small door open: one new place, or one second chance, each year.

Stay loyal. Let new things in, slowly.`}},m={己丑:{title:"Your progress tends to happen quietly, where nobody is watching.",gist:"You keep at things patiently and privately, though people may not know what you want or feel.",body:`{name}, there is a patient, private strength in you, like a field in winter: little seems to happen on top, while a lot gets ready underneath. You tend to keep at things steadily, without needing anyone to watch.

It might be a skill you practise for a few minutes a day, for months. Or a friendship you look after for years with small, regular check-ins.

The other side is that your feelings can stay underground too. People may not know what you want or what hurts until you say it, and once you choose a path, turning can be hard. Naming one small wish out loud each week is a good place to start.

Let one person see what is growing.`},己卯:{title:"Saying yes tends to come more easily to you than no.",gist:"You notice what others need and tend to help, though your own tiredness can go unseen.",body:`{name}, you tend to notice what each person around you needs, and once you have seen it, looking away is hard. Think of soil in spring with new shoots everywhere: each small green thing pulls at you, and you quietly feed it.

Maybe one favour arrives, then another, and you say yes to both before lunch. Or someone looks a little lost on their first day somewhere, and you are the one who walks over.

What can get hard is a tiredness you may not show. One sharp comment can stay longer than ten kind ones, and “no” may feel like the hardest word. One small habit: wait an hour before you answer a request, and check whether you have room.

Even good soil needs a season of rest.`},己巳:{title:"You like to understand things deeply, then pass them on warmly.",gist:"You learn deeply and share what you know warmly, though preparing can delay the start.",body:`{name}, your chart suggests a warm, thoughtful mind, like soil in the early summer sun. You tend to take things in slowly and turn them over until they are clear, then share them so others feel looked after.

It might be the clear notes friends ask to borrow, or a patient explanation that finally makes a hard idea clear for someone. Maybe it is the way you remember what helped you and pass it on to the next person.

The cost can be that preparing takes the place of beginning: one more book, one more opinion, one more good day to start. If you notice that, choose a small first step and give it a date.

You often know enough to begin.`},己未:{title:"“I’m fine” tends to come out before the full story does.",gist:"You handle hard things on your own and keep them private, so help can arrive late.",body:`{name}, you are self-reliant and steady, like a field at the end of summer: dry on top, strong underneath. You tend to manage hard things by yourself, and you keep a private world of thoughts that few people see.

Maybe a friend asks about your week, and you say “Fine, just busy” and ask about theirs. Or you work through a problem alone and mention it only once it is solved.

It can also mean that people cannot help with what they cannot see. When a lot stays inside for long, a small thing may bring out tears or anger that surprise everyone, you included. Telling one trusted person the true version while it is still small can help.

Being strong and asking for help can go together.`},己酉:{title:"You tend to show care by making things well.",gist:"You show love through careful, well-made things, though your eye for flaws can turn sharp.",body:`{name}, much of your care comes out through your hands. Like a field at harvest, what grows in you tends to become something people can hold, and you like it made properly, down to the details.

For some, it is a dish people ask about or a card made by hand. For others, a loose button fixed without being asked, or a day out planned so well that everyone can simply enjoy it.

The other side is your own high bar. When the small details go unnoticed, it can sting. On tired days, your eye for what is wrong may come out sharper than you meant. Try naming what you like about something first, then what you would change.

Make some things just for the joy of making them.`},己亥:{title:"Keeping things fair and careful tends to be how you show kindness.",gist:"You look after shared things carefully and fairly, though you may worry about there being enough.",body:`{name}, being practical and being kind tend to be the same thing for you. Like rich soil beside a winter river, you hold things for others, watch how much there is and make sure little goes to waste.

It can look like keeping track of who paid for what, then quietly making it lighter for a friend having a hard month. Or remembering whose turn it is, so nobody gives more than their share.

Sometimes this comes with a quiet worry about having enough, for you and for everyone. Strong feelings around you can also pull you along, so you agree to things you are unsure about. A short pause before you say yes can help.

Keep counting, and put your own name on the list.`},庚子:{title:"You tend to say out loud what others only think.",gist:"You speak plainly about what makes no sense, though the edge can hide the care behind it.",body:`{name}, a cool, clear mind sits behind your words, like a blade rinsed in cold water. You tend to see what makes no sense and say so plainly, often while others are still deciding whether to speak.

Maybe it is the question nobody else asks in a meeting or a class: “Who is this rule for?” Or a friend shows you a plan, and you point out the one weak spot while everyone else says it looks great.

The flip side is that the truth can arrive before the warmth. People may hear the edge and miss the care behind it. It can help to start with one thing you agree with, then say the hard part.

Clear words are easiest to hear when they come with kindness.`},庚寅:{title:"When something needs doing, you would rather start than wait.",gist:"You act quickly and get things moving, though you may rush others and forget to rest.",body:`{name}, picture an axe in a spring forest, happiest with work in front of it and open space ahead. You tend to be bold and active like that: you see what needs doing and move while others are still talking.

It might be booking the place after two weeks of talk that went nowhere. Or being outside, looking for the path, while everyone else is still unpacking.

Speed has a cost. You may rush people who need more time, or start the next thing before the last one is done, and your energy can come in big bursts, then run out. Try building one slow hour into a busy week, on purpose.

Not every tree needs cutting today.`},庚辰:{title:"When things go wrong, people tend to look to you.",gist:"You stay steady and lead when it counts, though following others or changing course can be hard.",body:`{name}, your chart points to a solid, commanding presence, like iron deep inside a mountain. When things go wrong, you tend to stay steady, decide quickly and say it clearly, and people around you feel safer.

Maybe the lights go out in a building, and you are the one with a torch and a plan. Or a group project falls apart a week before it is due, and you are the one who splits up the work.

The other side is a habit of doing things fully or not at all. Following someone else’s plan can be hard, and so can saying “I was wrong.” You might let someone else lead one small thing, and watch what they do with it.

Leading well includes knowing when to step back.`},庚午:{title:"Once you promise something, you tend to see it through.",gist:"You keep your word even on bad days, though rest can feel like something to earn.",body:`{name}, you hold your word close, like a sword shaped in a summer forge: heat tends to make you straighter, not weaker. Responsibility often sits well with you, and people tend to rely on you.

It might be turning up to a friend’s show on a rainy evening when others send “Sorry, next time.” Or finishing something you agreed to, late at night, when nobody would have minded if you asked for more time.

You can be very hard on yourself. You may carry duties nobody gave you, and rest can start to feel like something you have to earn. Try putting rest on your list like any other promise.

Even a good sword leaves the fire to cool.`},庚申:{title:"Doing things on your own, and winning, often comes naturally.",gist:"You are strong, independent and competitive, though asking for help can come too late.",body:`{name}, strength and independence sit at your centre, like steel set on steel: bright, hard and not made to bend. You tend to trust your own hands, and you enjoy a fair contest.

Maybe a board game turns serious the moment you read the rules. Or a new piece of furniture arrives, and it is built before anyone has offered to help.

It can also mean that asking for help comes late, even when you need it. Your honesty may sound harder than you mean, and the softness you show the few people close to you can go unseen by others. One small habit: ask for one small thing each week, and notice how it feels.

Not everything has to be a contest. Some things are better shared.`},庚戌:{title:"You tend to step in when someone is treated unfairly.",gist:"You stand up for people who are treated badly, though their fights can become your own.",body:`{name}, you are loyal and protective, like an iron gate on an autumn hill. You tend to guard the people inside, and watching someone be treated unfairly is very hard for you.

It can look like a calm “There’s no need to speak to them like that” when someone is rude to a person serving them. Or being the first friend to arrive when someone is in trouble.

The flip side is that other people’s fights can become your own. You may remember who hurt a friend long after the friend has let it go, and the world can start to look like only right and wrong. It can help to ask the person you are protecting what they want.

Keep guarding the gate, and open it sometimes too.`},辛丑:{title:"Your warmth tends to show slowly, to those who stay.",gist:"You are deep and loyal but open up slowly, so some people misread you as cold.",body:`{name}, there is a quiet depth in you, like a jewel under winter ground: the shine is real, just not on the surface. You tend to keep what matters close and show yourself a little at a time.

Maybe a friend learns only after a year how funny you are. Or you keep a few small things for years, a ticket or a note, each with a story you rarely tell.

What can get hard is that some people do not wait long enough, and they may read you as cold or distant. Old hurts can stay with you for a long time too. It is worth sharing one small, true thing a little earlier than feels natural.

You do not have to wait to be found.`},辛卯:{title:"You quickly see what to keep and what to let go.",gist:"Your good taste brings order to messy things, though the urge to edit can be hard to stop.",body:`{name}, you have a precise, tasteful eye, like garden shears in spring: everything around you grows in all directions, and you tend to give it shape. Messy things often become clear in your hands.

It might be a friend’s room that finally feels right after you move one piece of furniture. Or a long, messy plan that becomes three clear steps once you have looked at it.

The cost can be that editing is hard to stop. Plans, playlists and other people’s sentences may all start to look like drafts, and when you feel tense, your eye can get sharper still. Try asking “Do you want my ideas?” before you give them.

Trust your eye, and leave one corner of the garden wild.`},辛巳:{title:"Big moments tend to find you calm and well prepared.",gist:"You handle important moments with calm and care, though the work behind them often stays hidden.",body:`{name}, your chart suggests a polished, careful nature that shines when the light is on, like a jewel in bright sun. You tend to take important moments seriously and do them properly, and people often trust you with them.

Maybe it is a speech you rewrite several times so it sounds easy on the day. Or a dinner you host, where you look calm while you keep track of every detail.

Sometimes people see only the calm. You may feel watched even when nobody is looking, and being seen tired or unsure can be hard. Letting one person see a draft before it is finished can be a gentle start.

In the dark, a jewel can simply rest.`},辛未:{title:"Beauty tends to catch your eye where others walk past.",gist:"You find beauty in unexpected places, though feeling different can make you keep it to yourself.",body:`{name}, you have an artistic, sensitive eye, like a jewel in warm summer sand: not where anyone expects something precious, and shining anyway. Your taste tends to follow feeling more than trends.

It might be an old cup with a chipped rim that becomes your favourite thing. Or a song few people know, or a street others call plain that you find lovely.

The other side is that you may often feel a little different from the people around you. When a choice is not understood, you might stop sharing that part of yourself, and small worries can stay for days. Showing one thing you love to one curious person is a small way back.

Your own kind of shine is the point.`},辛酉:{title:"You tend to notice the one small thing that is off.",gist:"Your high standards make your work exact, though little feels finished and care can look cold.",body:`{name}, your chart points to a clean, exact nature, like a cut jewel in a fine setting. Your standards tend to be high and your own, and you notice the one small detail that is not quite right.

Maybe wrapping paper whose pattern does not meet at the join means starting again. Or a document gets one more check after everyone else has called it done.

It can also mean that almost nothing feels finished. You may be as strict with others as with yourself, and asking for help can feel hard. To some people, your precision may look cold, though it is a way of caring. Try deciding what “good enough” means before you start.

A jewel does not have to be perfect to catch the light.`},辛亥:{title:"The words you choose tend to be the ones people keep.",gist:"You turn feelings into clear, true words, though hurt can make them sharper than you mean.",body:`{name}, you feel things finely, like a jewel washed in a cold, clean stream, and you can turn those feelings into clear, true words. What you say tends to stay with people.

This might show up as a few lines on a goodbye card that someone rereads for years. Or a message on a friend’s hard day that says exactly what they needed to hear.

The cost can be that sad things reach you just as finely. On grey days, a soft sadness may settle in, and when you are hurt, your words can cut deeper than you meant. One small habit: wait until morning before you send a reply written while hurt.

Your words are a gift. Keep them kind, to others and to yourself.`},壬子:{title:"Your mind tends to roam far, and it needs room.",gist:"Your restless mind needs freedom, though others may not always follow where it goes.",body:`{name}, your chart suggests a big, deep, free mind, like the open sea at midnight in winter: calm on top, huge underneath. You tend to need room to think and move, the way others need sleep.

It might be a late night that starts with one question and ends with a plan for life somewhere else. Or a sudden wish to walk for hours with nowhere in particular to go.

Sometimes people cannot follow. You may go quiet for a while, then come back with your mind changed, and strong feelings can rise all at once. A short note like “I need a few quiet days” keeps one or two people close while you are away.

Stay free, and let a few people see how deep the water goes.`},壬寅:{title:"Ideas you share freely tend to grow in other hands.",gist:"You share ideas and energy freely, though you can give until there is little left.",body:`{name}, you are generous and full of ideas, like a spring river running through a young forest: things tend to turn green where you pass. You share what you know without counting.

Maybe a half-joking idea over coffee becomes a gathering that others want to repeat. Or a tip you once gave a friend turns into something they now do every week.

The flip side is that you may start more than you can carry, and your mind can move on as soon as something grows. You can also give your energy away until you feel empty. It can help to hand one project to someone else once it has taken root.

Rivers need rain too. Let others give to you.`},壬辰:{title:"Big, hard goals tend to bring out your best.",gist:"You work best toward big, hard goals, though ordinary days and rest can feel too small.",body:`{name}, think of a deep lake held behind a mountain wall: great power, kept under control. Pressure tends to focus you rather than stop you, and you often do your best work when the goal is far away and a little impossible.

Perhaps it is a plan others call too big, which you quietly start that same week. Or a skill that takes years, which you enjoy more the harder it gets.

The other side is a pull toward extremes. Ordinary days may feel too small, rest can feel like lost time, and when things move slowly, the calm surface can break. Try counting rest as part of the plan, not a pause from it.

Keep the big dream, and let the quiet days count too.`},壬午:{title:"Your cool head and warm heart tend to take turns.",gist:"You can be careful and generous, calm and warm, and the two sides may pull against each other.",body:`{name}, two sides take turns in you, like the sea at noon in summer: clear and cool underneath, bright and sparkling on top. You tend to be careful with what you have and warm with people.

Maybe you compare prices for something small, then buy a friend a gift without a second look. Or one week you want to stay home and plan, and the next you want to go somewhere new with everyone.

This can feel like a small pull in two directions inside. Your moods may shift like light on water, and people may find it hard to guess which side they will meet. A simple step: tell the people close to you which side is leading today.

Both halves are you. Let them take turns, kindly.`},壬申:{title:"One small question can send your mind exploring for hours.",gist:"You learn fast and follow your curiosity far, though many paths can end half-explored.",body:`{name}, a quick, playful mind is one of your gifts, like a mountain river near its source, always finding a new way down. You tend to learn by following one question to the next.

Picture a strange fact mentioned at lunch that turns into an evening of videos and links sent to friends. Or a new tool you work out by trying every button before anyone opens the guide.

The cost can be that many streams never reach the sea. A list of half-learned skills may grow long, and at night your mind can keep running when your body wants to sleep. One idea: pick one interest each season and follow it all the way.

Curiosity is a gift. Now and then, give one stream your whole attention.`},壬戌:{title:"You tend to keep quiet watch over the people you love.",gist:"You guard the people you love quietly and closely, though your worry often goes unseen.",body:`{name}, your care runs deep and watchful, like a mountain lake in autumn: still on top, held by rock, much deeper than it looks. You tend to look after the people you love quietly and completely.

Maybe it is the “Text me when you’re home” you send after an evening out, and the phone you keep close until every reply arrives. Or remembering a friend’s big day and checking in that morning.

What can weigh on you is worry that nobody sees, and it is hard to put down. When someone hurts a person you love, the still water may turn dark and take a long time to settle. Sharing the watching with someone means it is not all yours to carry.

Let someone keep watch over you too.`},癸丑:{title:"Hard moments tend to make you calmer, not louder.",gist:"You stay calm and find a way through difficulty, so people may forget to ask how you are.",body:`{name}, there is a quiet toughness in you, like cold rain on a winter field: steady, patient, still falling long after everyone has gone inside. Difficulty tends to make you calm rather than loud.

It might be a cancelled late train, when others crowd the desk and you are already looking up the night bus. Or a plan that falls apart, with a second one ready before anyone asks.

It can also mean that coping becomes a habit. People may assume you are fine and stop asking, and feelings held in for long can come out suddenly, stronger than anyone expects. Telling one person when a day was hard, even after you handled it, keeps that door open.

Getting through is a skill. So is asking for company.`},癸卯:{title:"Your small kindnesses tend to help other people grow.",gist:"Your gentle encouragement helps others grow, though your own plans may wait in a drawer.",body:`{name}, you are gentle and encouraging, like soft spring rain on new shoots: quiet and light, and things often look greener after you pass. Your kindness tends to arrive at the right moment.

Maybe a short, warm comment you left on someone’s first attempt at something is still remembered years later. Or a friend tries a new thing because you said, “You would be good at that.”

The flip side is that you may water everyone’s garden but your own, and your own ideas can wait in a drawer. In a disagreement, you may stay quiet more often than you would like. Try giving one of your own plans the encouragement you give others.

Save a little rain for your own garden.`},癸巳:{title:"Problems tend to find quiet solutions in your hands.",gist:"You quietly find ways around problems, though people may not see the effort behind it.",body:`{name}, your chart suggests a bright, resourceful cleverness, like rain falling while the sun shines: light, surprising, sometimes leaving a rainbow. You tend to know where to look and who to ask.

It might be a sold-out event that you still find a fair way into. Or a form everyone else gave up on, finished after one calm phone call.

Sometimes people think it all comes easily. They may not see the planning, or the small worries you carry while you solve things, and always being the one who fixes it can be tiring. Try saying “I’m not sure, can you look?” now and then.

Some puzzles can belong to someone else.`},癸未:{title:"When a room gets tense, you tend to cool it down.",gist:"You ease tension and bring relief to others, though you may take in their stress.",body:`{name}, you have a soothing, kind way about you, like rain on a dry field at the end of summer: things tend to soften and breathe again near you. You often notice when people are tired or tense before they say so.

It can look like a hot, slow afternoon when two friends start to argue, and you move everyone into the shade and get them laughing. Or a long queue where a small joke from you makes the wait lighter.

The cost can be that dry ground drinks all the rain. You may take in other people’s stress until little is left for you. It can help to name, at the end of a day, one thing you need.

Let someone bring the cool water to you too.`},癸酉:{title:"Whether something feels right is often clear to you quickly.",gist:"Your quick, clear sense of places and people guides you, though you may close doors fast.",body:`{name}, your chart points to a clear, quiet intuition, like spring water running between rocks, so clear you can see the bottom. You tend to take in more than you can put into words.

Maybe you walk into a new place and know within a minute that it is not for you, without being able to say why. Or you meet someone and feel at ease, or not, long before there is any reason.

The other side is that a door can close quickly, on places and on people, and some may read you as picky or distant. Now and then, give a second look to one thing your first feeling said no to.

Trust your clear water, and let a new stream join it now and then.`},癸亥:{title:"Stories, songs and other people’s moods tend to reach you deeply.",gist:"You feel things deeply and imagine widely, though other people’s sadness can stay with you.",body:`{name}, you are open and wide, like rain falling into a winter sea: it can be hard to tell where you end and the world begins. You tend to feel stories, songs and other people’s moods almost as your own.

It might be a film, or one small character in it, that you keep thinking about for days. Or a stranger’s bad day, seen on a bus, that you carry all evening.

The cost can be taking in a lot. Sadness that is not yours may linger, and you may drift so far inside that plans, messages and time slip away. A small closing habit at night may help, like writing down one thing that was yours today.

Feel all of it. Even the sea has a shore.`}},c={peach:{title:"People tend to remember you after meeting you only once.",gist:"Your easy charm makes you memorable, though it can bring attention you did not ask for.",body:`Tradition links this trait with easy charm and with being remembered. You tend to leave an impression without trying: something in how you listen, laugh or turn towards people.

It might be a café where they start your order after two visits, or someone at a wedding who still knows your name a year later. Maybe in a group project, people come to you first. Work with people, such as hosting, teaching or performing, may suit you.

The other side is attention you did not ask for. Some people may decide who you are from one smile. It can help to keep a few clear, friendly words ready for when you want space.

Your charm is yours. You choose where it goes.`},horse:{title:"New places tend to wake you up rather than scare you.",gist:"You learn new places and changes fast, though staying still for long can make you restless.",body:`This trait is traditionally linked with movement: travel, moves and change. You tend to settle in quickly somewhere new, and change often gives you energy rather than taking it.

Maybe by your second day in a new city you know a shortcut and a cheap place for lunch. Or a new class, team or neighbourhood feels familiar within a week. Work that moves, or a life between languages, may suit you.

The flip side can be restlessness when life stays still for a while. You may feel an urge to change something big when a small change would do. Try a small one first: a new route, a new dish, a day somewhere you have never been.

Go far. Keep in touch with the people who stay.`},canopy:{title:"Your deepest work often happens in a quiet corner.",gist:"You focus deeply when alone, though you may drift from friends or keep your work hidden.",body:`You tend to think deeply, work best alone and stay with one thing for hours. Tradition ties this trait to art, solitude and a quiet, inward mind.

It might be a drawing, a song, a puzzle or a big question about life. Maybe you look up and it is dark outside, with cold tea and a few unread messages.

Sometimes you can disappear for a while. Friends may wonder if something is wrong when you are simply busy inside, and showing your work can feel harder than making it. Sharing one small piece with one person you trust is a good start.

Keep your corner. Leave the door open a little.`},nobleman:{title:"You accept help warmly, and people often like helping you.",gist:"People often enjoy helping you, though you may wait too long before trying alone.",body:`This trait is pictured as help that arrives when it is needed: an image, not a promise. In you, it can show as a way of receiving help: you tend to ask clearly, say thank you and mean it, and people often feel good helping you.

Maybe a stranger offers a charger when your phone dies, or someone shows you around in your first week somewhere new. Teachers, neighbours or people you barely know may seem to turn up at the right time.

The one thing to watch is waiting. If you are used to help arriving, it can be easy to wait a little long before trying alone. Try taking the first step yourself, then asking.

Accept help warmly. Then be the help for someone else.`},blade:{title:"You often play to win, even when it is just for fun.",gist:"You compete fiercely and act when others hesitate, though arguments can turn into contests.",body:`You tend to step forward when others hesitate, and once you are in, you give it everything. This trait is linked with a fierce, brave and competitive energy.

It might be a board game that was meant to be relaxed, a quiz night or a debate in class. It can also look like speaking up for someone who is treated unfairly. Sport, competition or anything that needs courage may suit you.

The flip side is that a disagreement can turn into a contest, and your words may land harder than you meant. Before you reply in an argument, it can help to ask yourself: do I want to win, or to be understood?

Save your fire for what matters most.`},kuigang:{title:"Hard moments often bring out your clearest, boldest self.",gist:"You lead well in hard moments and give everything, though ordinary days may feel flat.",body:`Tradition reads this trait as strong leadership and an all-or-nothing way of meeting life. You tend to be at your best when things are at their worst: clear, bold and ready to lead.

Maybe a plan falls apart two days before an event, and while the group chat panics, you calmly make a new one. Or the project others call impossible is exactly the one that draws you in. Leading, organising or turning things around may suit you.

What can feel harder is the middle. Ordinary days may feel flat, and small problems may get your full force. Try giving small things a small response, and saving the big push for big moments.

Give everything when it matters. On quiet days, ordinary is enough.`},scholar:{title:"You tend to learn quickly and make confusing things clear.",gist:"You learn fast and explain things clearly, though more study can feel safer than the next step.",body:`You tend to pick things up quickly, read carefully, remember what you read and find the mistake others missed. The trait is linked with learning, words and paperwork.

Maybe friends ask you to check an important message before they press send. Or a form full of tiny boxes, a cover letter or a set of class notes tends to end up with you. Exams, writing, teaching or work where details matter may suit you.

Learning can also become a safe place to stay. One more course or one more article can feel easier than the step you are ready to take. Try setting a date to use what you already know.

Learn as much as you like. Then let it out into the world.`},moonVirtue:{title:"People often forgive you quickly, and you forgive them too.",gist:"Your kindness tends to be met with kindness, though you may forgive fast and hide your hurt.",body:`Traditionally, this trait is linked with kindness that is returned, and with being forgiven easily. You tend to share, notice and not judge, and people often give that softness back.

It might be a late birthday message answered with a heart and a joke about cake. Or friends who come to you after a fight, because you can calm things down without taking sides.

The flip side is that you may forgive too fast. You might smile after a sharp word and never say it hurt. Naming it once, calmly, can help, even if you have already forgiven.

Stay kind, and include yourself.`},heavenVirtue:{title:"You often stay calm while others are still worrying.",gist:"Your calm makes problems feel smaller, though you may leave the backup plan for later.",body:`The traditional image of this trait is quiet protection: trouble that passes by. That is an image, not a guarantee. What it often shows in you is a steady calm. You tend not to panic, and problems can look smaller once you have faced them.

Maybe you are the friend who says, “Let’s see what happens,” while the rest of the group is already worried. Or a week of worry about a mistake ends with a shrug. People may come to you in a hard moment just to feel calmer.

Calm can also turn into leaving things late. One small habit: keep a simple plan B for the things that matter.

Trust your calm. Carry the umbrella anyway.`},whiteTiger:{title:"When something needs doing fast, you are often first to move.",gist:"You are quick and clear when things get intense, though speed can become a habit.",body:`This trait is known for an intense, strong presence. You tend to bring energy into a room, and when something needs doing fast, your mind goes clear and your hands get busy.

Maybe a glass breaks and you are already holding the broom while others are still saying, “Oh no.” Or a busy shift, a match or a tight deadline is where you feel most awake.

The other side is that fast can become your usual pace for everything, even big choices that would go better slowly. It can help to give a big decision one night’s sleep before you say yes.

Your speed is a gift. You choose when to use it.`}},w=[{title:"You often learn best by trying things yourself.",gist:"You learn by doing and trust your judgement, though you may take the long way rather than ask.",body:`Independence comes naturally to you. You tend to learn by trying, trust your own judgement and get going without waiting for permission. A friendly rival, someone a step ahead, can make you even better.

It might be furniture built without the instructions, a recipe learned from a video and a few tries, or a new skill picked up by simply starting. Maybe one screw is left over, and the shelf still stands.

Sometimes you take the long way. You may keep going alone when one question would save an hour. Try asking early, while it still feels optional.

Your way stays yours, even with help.`},{title:"You tend to turn ordinary moments into something people enjoy.",gist:"You make, speak and perform with ease, though finishing can feel dull and criticism can sting.",body:`Expression is where you shine: making, speaking and showing. You tend to turn what you see and feel into something others can enjoy, and you may question rules that make no sense.

It might be a story about a queue at the post office that has everyone laughing. Or a dish, a song, a drawing, a video or a better way to run a meeting. You might enjoy creative work, teaching or anything with an audience.

Starting is often more fun than finishing, though, and criticism can sting, because what you make feels personal. Try finishing one small thing before you start the next, and sharing it with someone kind.

Make things, and let people see them.`},{title:"You can often see what things are really worth.",gist:"You have a practical eye for value and results, though even rest can become a calculation.",body:`Your chart leans towards the practical. You tend to see what something is worth, what a plan really needs, and how to get results with what you have.

Maybe you spot the good lamp under the dust at a flea market, or find the trip that is cheaper and somehow nicer. Or you are the one who notices that a plan has three steps nobody needs. Trade, planning or running things may suit you.

The flip side is that everything can become a calculation, even free time. An afternoon spent on nothing may feel like a waste. Some time with no purpose at all can count as time well spent.

Your eye for value is a gift. Your own time is worth a lot, too.`},{title:"People often trust you with things that matter.",gist:"You are responsible and steady under pressure, though rest without a task can feel strange.",body:`Structure and responsibility suit you. You tend to do what you said you would, properly and on time, and pressure often makes you calmer rather than weaker.

It might be a neighbour who leaves you their keys and a page of notes about the cat, or a group that asks you to lead while the leader is away. Maybe you are the one who keeps the important list. Roles with clear structure and real responsibility may suit you.

Sometimes you carry too much. You can be hard on yourself, and sometimes on others, when things are not done right. Rest can go on your list, as a task like any other.

Keep your promises. Make one to yourself, too.`},{title:"You tend to understand things deeply, sometimes too deeply.",gist:"You understand deeply and see the real problem fast, though one choice may turn into weeks of research.",body:`Your chart leans towards learning and reflection. You tend to understand things deeply, connect ideas and sense what is going on under the surface. Support and good teachers often seem to find a learner like you.

Maybe one question over dinner becomes two hours of reading. Or a friend talks about a problem for an hour, and you name the real problem in one sentence. Research, teaching or advice could be a good fit.

Thinking can also turn into over-thinking. One choice may turn into weeks of research, with forty tabs still open at two in the morning. A time limit can help: choose with what you already know.

You already know more than you think.`}],f={"0:0":{title:"A friend’s good news can wake up your own plans.",gist:"You grow best beside equals, though friends can turn into races and asking for help feels hard.",body:`You tend to grow best next to equals. Someone doing well does not make you smaller; it wakes you up. Work that is truly yours to grow suits you, alone or in a team of peers, and being checked at every step can drain you fast.

It might be a classmate who finishes a course, or a friend who starts something of their own. You are glad for them, and on the way home you may find yourself adding a goal or two.

The flip side is that friends can turn into races, and races are tiring. Asking for help can also feel harder than it should. Sharing one goal with a friend, instead of racing them, can help.

Trees in a forest grow taller beside their neighbours.`},"0:1":{title:"Friendly contests with equals tend to light you up.",gist:"A little friendly competition gives you energy, though slow days bore you and losses can sting.",body:`Your chart points to a bright, competitive warmth that grows next to people at your level. A team of equals who push each other tends to suit you, and you like your effort to be seen, not lost in a long list of names.

It can look like a quiet afternoon that turns into a small game: who finishes first, who finds the best idea. Maybe the prize is a sticker or a snack, and everyone works faster while laughing.

The flip side is that slow days with nobody to play along can bore you, and a small loss can sting more than it should. Count your own progress, not only the score.

Your fire grows when you share it. Let someone else win now and then.`},"0:2":{title:"You tend to trust the ways you built yourself.",gist:"People lean on your steady, self-made ways, though your way can slowly become the only way.",body:`You tend to trust what you have built with your own hands. You work well as an equal: a partner, a co-owner, the one who runs their own corner. People lean on you because you do not shake easily, and change pushed on you for no clear reason can wear you down.

Maybe it is a paper list you keep after everyone moves to a new app, and yours stays up to date. Or a way of sorting things that others laughed at, until they asked to copy it.

The flip side is that “my way” can slowly become “the only way”. If you find yourself saying no before trying, give the new thing one honest week.

Mountains do not move, but the paths on them can.`},"0:3":{title:"Your own high standard is often stricter than anyone’s.",gist:"Your sharp eye wants things done right, though fixing everyone’s parts can leave you carrying it all.",body:`You have a sharp eye and a high standard of your own. Work you can own from start to finish tends to suit you, next to skilled peers who speak plainly. A clear task, a clear deadline and nobody checking on you: that is a good day.

It might be a shared file the night before it is due, where you quietly fix the layout and check every number. Or a team task that ends up back in your hands, finished your way.

The flip side is carrying the load alone, then feeling tired and a little annoyed. When you fix everything, others miss the chance to learn. Try giving one note instead of one rewrite.

A blade stays sharp when it is not used for everything.`},"0:4":{title:"Your best work often comes on your own schedule.",gist:"You need freedom to work your own way, though days without edges can drift and cost sleep.",body:`Your chart suggests a mind that needs its own current. You like people, but you tend to work best your own way: your hours, your route, a few clever peers to share ideas with, and nobody watching every move.

It might be ideas that arrive late in the evening, or halfway through a long walk with no plan. Maybe you finish in two quiet hours what a group spent two days planning.

The flip side is drifting. Without some edges, days can spill everywhere, and sleep is often the first thing to go. One fixed point in the day lets the rest flow around it.

A river needs banks to reach the sea.`},"1:0":{title:"You can make a hard idea simple for someone else.",gist:"You love teaching and starting new things, though you may lose interest in the slow middle.",body:`Your chart points to someone who thinks out loud and loves to watch things grow, people included. Your ideas want to become something real: a lesson, a plan, a sketch that helps. Rules that make no sense tend to get a polite question from you.

It might be a napkin with three boxes and an arrow that makes a friend’s problem clear. Or a younger student or a new teammate who suddenly says, “Why did nobody explain it like that?”

The flip side is a long list of fresh starts. You may begin five things with joy and drift away in the slow middle. If you recognise that, pick one to carry to the end this season.

A tree in blossom still needs its roots.`},"1:1":{title:"A simple task in your hands can turn into a show.",gist:"You come alive with an audience and room to play, but too many stages can leave you empty.",body:`Your chart suggests someone made to be seen and heard. Your energy wants a shape: a talk, a dish, an event, anything that turns warmth into something people can enjoy. An audience and room to play bring out your best, and long, quiet work behind a closed door can dim you.

It might be five plain slides that gain a story at the start and a picture that makes the room laugh. Or a small birthday that becomes a theme night once you join the planning.

The flip side is burning too hot. Saying yes to every stage can leave some weeks with nothing left, even for you. Keep one evening a week with no audience at all.

A fire that is looked after burns all night.`},"1:2":{title:"You can make something useful out of very little.",gist:"Your patient hands make things that work and last, though others may get the thanks.",body:`Your talent tends to be practical and patient. You show who you are by making things that work and last. Busy hands and time to do it properly suit you, whether that means making, cooking, fixing, growing or teaching step by step. Rushed work you cannot finish well can drain you.

Maybe it is a sign made from an old box and a marker. Maybe it is a shelf that appears just when the snacks need a place, or a good meal from whatever is left in the kitchen.

The flip side is that you may hand your work over quietly, and others get the thanks. One plain sentence about what you made is enough.

Treasure waits inside the mountain. Let people see yours.`},"1:3":{title:"You often see the mistake everyone else missed.",gist:"You spot mistakes and say so plainly, though honest notes can feel cold to others.",body:`You have a clear eye and a clear voice. When a plan has a hole, you tend to say so, politely but plainly, and often with a better version ready. Work where precision is the art may suit you: editing, design, building, any craft that must be exactly right.

It might be a missing letter on a poster that ten people already approved. Or one step in a plan that cannot work as written, which you see on the first read.

The flip side is that your truth can feel cold to others. Not every draft needs every note on the first day. If people go quiet, start with what works.

A bell sounds beautiful because it rings clear. Ring it gently.`},"1:4":{title:"Ideas tend to arrive faster than you can write them down.",gist:"Your ideas flow and connect easily, though routine work can feel grey and deadlines arrive fast.",body:`Your mind tends to flow and connect. Ideas may come in the shower or on the bus, and they want to be shared. Work where words and ideas are the point may suit you: writing, explaining, planning something new, telling a story that moves people.

It might be a wall of sticky notes where half are yours. Or a group chat where you share a song, a scene from a film and a question nobody asked, and one of them turns out to be the answer.

The flip side is a mind full of beginnings. Routine work can feel slow and grey, and deadlines can come faster than you expect. Write every idea down, then choose one to finish.

Rain helps a garden only where it lands.`},"2:0":{title:"You tend to grow small ideas into results you can count.",gist:"You build small ideas into real results step by step, though a slow week can feel like failure.",body:`You tend to see where things can grow, and you like to watch results grow with them. A clear goal and a way to measure it suit you: people helped, pages written, things sold. Vague goals can make you lose interest.

It might be a hobby that slowly turns into a small stall or a few paid lessons. Or a simple sheet where you track one number you care about, and check it more often than you planned.

The flip side is counting everything, even rest. A slow week can feel like failure when it is only a season. Before you judge a week, look back over a whole month.

Trees grow one ring at a time.`},"2:1":{title:"Your warmth often turns into quick, visible results.",gist:"Your warmth gets people to say yes, though you may chase the next win before enjoying this one.",body:`Your chart points to warmth that turns into results. People tend to trust your energy, and you like a clear goal and a number that moves. Pitching, selling, organising events or any team that celebrates its wins out loud may suit you.

It might be asking ten local shops for prizes for a fair, and remembering every owner’s name. Or a fundraiser where you tell the story so well that people give twice.

The flip side is chasing the next number before you enjoy the last one. A slow project with no finish line can make you restless. Try marking each win with something small before you move on.

A flame warms best when it stays a while.`},"2:2":{title:"People often ask you where things are, and you know.",gist:"You look after supplies, schedules and shared things well, though the small jobs tend to land on you.",body:`You tend to look after resources the way a farmer looks after water: nothing wasted, everything where it is needed. Real things to manage may suit you, like supplies, schedules, a shop floor, a household or a project with many pieces. Mess and waste can wear you down.

It might be the question “Do we have more paper?” coming to you first. Or knowing that the spare key is in the blue tin and the big pot is on the top shelf.

The flip side is that the small jobs land on you, and it may go unnoticed until you are away for a week. Show one person where the key is.

It is a gift to you both.`},"2:3":{title:"You tend to see what matters and leave the rest.",gist:"You go straight for a clear result, though people who like to talk things through may feel rushed.",body:`Your chart suggests a clear, practical mind that sees what matters and lets the rest go. Work with a clear result at the end tends to suit you: a deal agreed, an order sent, a room cleared, a price settled. Tasks that never end can make you tense.

It might be a long meeting, or a chat about a trip that keeps coming back to the same point, until you write three lines: what we decided, who does what, by when. Everyone looks relieved.

The flip side is impatience. People who need to talk things through can feel cut off, and some value is slow and hard to count. If someone goes quiet, ask one more question.

Before you cut, listen once more.`},"2:4":{title:"You often know who needs what, and who has it.",gist:"You connect people with what they need, though chasing every small chance can keep any from growing.",body:`You tend to see how things flow: people, chances, and things nobody is using. Results reach you the way water finds low ground, through people and good timing. Work that moves between people may suit you, like trading, matching or planning. The same task every day can feel like a cage.

Maybe one friend needs a bike and another has a spare one in the hall. Or someone needs a tutor, and you know who just passed the same exam. Two messages, and both have what they need.

The flip side is following every small chance, so none of them grows big. Choose a few to keep.

Small streams become a river when they join.`},"3:0":{title:"People tend to look to you when someone must take charge.",gist:"People trust you to lead under pressure, though you can be hard on yourself and rarely rest.",body:`You tend to take responsibility and stand straight under pressure, like a young tree. Clear rules and a clear path upward do not scare you; they help you grow. Fair teams with clear structure suit you, and rules that only some people follow can drain you.

It might be the moment a teacher leaves the room, or a group leader is running late, and people turn to you. You may not have asked for it, but you check the list and keep things moving.

The flip side is being hard on yourself. A small mistake can feel big, and you may rarely put the weight down. Try ending the day by naming one thing that went well.

A trimmed tree grows stronger. It does not need trimming every day.`},"3:1":{title:"When things get busy, you tend to grow calm and warm.",gist:"Pressure tends to make you calm, clear and warm, though quiet days can feel strangely flat.",body:`Pressure tends to feed your flame instead of putting it out. A role with real weight and clear rules may suit you, where people count on you. You can be the steady leader with a warm voice that a busy place needs.

It might be a rush hour when the card machine stops and the phone keeps ringing. Or the night before an event, when nothing is ready. Maybe for a second your heart races, then you give everyone a job and tell one small joke.

The flip side is that calm days can feel flat, and rest may not come easily when nothing is urgent. Give quiet days a small goal of their own.

A lantern burns best behind glass. Some structure can protect your flame.`},"3:2":{title:"You tend to be the one people know they can count on.",gist:"You carry duty well, though you may keep supporting places that do not support you back.",body:`Your chart suggests someone who carries duty well. Promises, rules and routines feel like solid ground, and you tend to make that ground safe for others. Places that need someone steady and trusted may suit you, and fair rules and loyal people bring out your best.

It can look like turning up on a cold, wet morning when others stay home. Or being the first name people check when they plan a busy week, a team schedule or a care plan.

The flip side is staying too long. You may keep supporting places or people that do not support you back. If you notice that, ask what you would tell a friend in the same place.

Roots hold the hill in place. Make sure someone looks after yours too.`},"3:3":{title:"Strict standards tend to sharpen you rather than break you.",gist:"Clear standards and real tests make you better, though you can be harsh with yourself and others.",body:`You tend to grow under pressure the way steel is shaped in fire. Clear standards, fair rules and real tests make you better, and you respect people who expect a lot. Work where precision and duty matter may suit you. Careless leadership can frustrate you.

It might be a page that comes back covered in red ink, or a coach who asks for the same move ten more times. Where others feel hurt, you may go home, try again and come back sharper.

The flip side is being harsh, with yourself first and then with others. Not everyone grows best in the heat. Try one kind word before the correction.

Steel is made in heat, then cooled. You need both.`},"3:4":{title:"A clear deadline can give your mind speed and direction.",gist:"Deadlines and clear roles give your ideas direction, though waiting for pressure can cost sleep and quality.",body:`Your mind tends to be wide and free, and like water, it moves faster with a clear path. A deadline, a clear role or a rule that makes sense gives your ideas speed. Big, complex places may suit you, where someone must understand the whole system. Rules with no reason can drain you.

Maybe with a week to spare, you tidy your desk and read about octopuses. Then, an hour before the deadline, every sentence comes out right.

The flip side is waiting for pressure instead of choosing a pace. Last-minute work can cost sleep, and sometimes quality. Small deadlines of your own, like a start time or a first page, can help.

Give your mind a path, then let it flow.`},"4:0":{title:"You like to learn everything you can before you begin.",gist:"You learn deeply and ask sharp questions, though you may study a thing for months before trying it.",body:`Your chart points to someone who learns deeply and grows with good teachers. Knowledge tends to grow slowly in you into something useful. Studying, teaching, research or guiding others may suit you. A place where questions are welcome brings out your best, and work that never lets you ask why can drain you.

It might be reading every page about a new course or role before the first day. Or asking a question in the first week that makes someone stop and say, “Good point. I never thought of that.”

The flip side is waiting to feel ready. You can read about a thing for months before you let yourself try it. Take one small step while you are still learning.

Roots and branches grow at the same time.`},"4:1":{title:"A kind word from someone you respect can carry you far.",gist:"Encouragement from people you respect lights you up, though silence can make you doubt good work.",body:`You tend to burn on good fuel: ideas, encouragement and people who believe in you. With someone wise nearby, you are bright and warm, and you pass the light on. Places where you can keep learning and teach what you learn may suit you. Somewhere with nothing new to learn can leave you cold.

It might be five words from a teacher or a more experienced colleague: “This part is really good.” You may walk home lighter and work for weeks as if someone lit a lamp inside you.

The flip side is that silence can feel like failure. When nobody says anything, you may doubt work that is actually fine. Write down one thing you did well today.

Your own light counts too.`},"4:2":{title:"You tend to think slowly and bring back answers people trust.",gist:"Your careful thinking brings answers people trust, though with too much time, worry can take over.",body:`You tend to take things in slowly and turn them over, the way soil takes in sun and rain. What comes out is solid and kind, and people trust it. Advising, teaching, caring or planning may suit you. Being rushed into big choices can drain you.

It might be saying, “Give me until tomorrow,” when everyone wants a decision now. Then you bring back one calm page that answers questions nobody had asked yet.

The flip side is worry. Given too long, the same thoughts can circle, and one night can become a week. If you notice that, set a day to decide before you start thinking.

Warm soil grows good things once the seed goes in.`},"4:3":{title:"You can become the quiet expert on what others avoid.",gist:"Quiet study makes you a careful expert, though you may polish your work instead of showing it.",body:`You love to understand things properly. Quiet time to study tends to make you a specialist, careful and exact. Research, checking, technical work or anything that rewards careful reading may suit you. A calm team that respects knowledge brings out your best, and noise and quick, shallow answers can tire you.

It might be the old machine nobody understands, a long official form or a strange rule everyone avoids. One quiet afternoon with it, and people start coming to you.

The flip side is hiding in the details. You can polish and check for a long time and keep work from people until it is perfect. Show it when it is good enough to talk about.

A jewel shines only after it leaves the ground.`},"4:4":{title:"You often notice small signs before others see a problem.",gist:"You notice small signs early and join them into a pattern, though you may doubt your own sense.",body:`You tend to read what lies under the surface. Your mind works deep and quiet, joining small signs into a pattern. Room to think and listen may suit you: research, care, planning or advice, any work where seeing early matters more than speaking loudly. Noise and constant chatter can tire you.

It might be a small change in someone’s voice, or a number that is fine but feels wrong. Maybe you mention it gently, and later someone remembers you saw it first.

The flip side is living deep inside your own head, and doubting the very feeling that is often right. Writing it down and checking it later can help.

A deep well gives clean water. Trust what comes up from yours.`}},p={self:{...g,...m},seenOutside:d,seenHome:y,energy:t,energyMissing:n,love:h,lovePlace:l,arguments:o,friends:s,family:a,work:f,money:u,talents:c,talentsByGroup:w,home:i,homeHorse:r,advice:e};export{p as story};
